values
  ('lituk:S1.1-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, someone applying for permanent residence or citizenship agrees to respect the UK''s:',array['sports, music and food','laws, values and traditions','weather, landscape and wildlife','banks, shops and businesses']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Applicants accept the responsibilities of permanent residence and agree to respect the country''s laws, values and traditions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does passing the Life in the UK test help to show, according to the handbook?',array['That you are entitled to free private healthcare','That you have lived in the UK for at least ten years','That you are prepared to settle permanently in the UK','That you are qualified to work in the civil service']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The test is one part of showing that a person is ready to settle permanently in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The handbook says Britain is proud of its record of:',array['never being invaded','having no written laws','welcoming new migrants','keeping one religion']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The introduction says the UK is proud of how it has welcomed new migrants.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, what do new migrants add to the UK''s national life?',array['Wealth and power','Diversity and dynamism','Tradition and ceremony','Order and discipline']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The introduction says newcomers bring diversity and dynamism to national life.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The handbook describes applying to become a permanent resident or citizen as an important decision and commitment.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Applying for permanent residence or citizenship is described as a significant decision and a commitment.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which fields does the handbook say British people have been at the heart of world development?',array['Military, religious, agricultural and financial','Political, scientific, industrial and cultural','Sporting, culinary, naval and colonial','Legal, medical, musical and commercial']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The introduction names political, scientific, industrial and cultural development as areas where Britain''s people have been central.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides preparing for the test, the handbook aims to help new residents:',array['learn to drive and buy a home','find a job and open a bank account','integrate into society and play a full role locally','choose a school and register with a doctor']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The handbook is meant to help readers integrate and take a full part in their local community.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The handbook aims to give readers a broad general knowledge of the UK''s:',array['economy, industry and trade','cookery, fashion and music','climate, rivers and wildlife','culture, laws and history']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): One aim of the handbook is a broad general knowledge of British culture, laws and history.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.1-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How does the handbook describe Britain''s history?',array['Recent and uneventful','Short and peaceful','Long and illustrious','Quiet and isolated']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The opening of the handbook describes Britain''s history as long and illustrious, and its society as modern and thriving.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, the questions in the Life in the UK test require an understanding of English at which level?',array['ESOL Level 2','ESOL Entry Level 1','ESOL Entry Level 3','GCSE grade A']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The test is written so that it needs English at ESOL Entry Level 3.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: According to the handbook, applications for permanent residence or citizenship can be submitted free of charge.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): A fee must be paid to submit an application, and the amount varies by application type.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, what decides which application form and evidence you must provide?',array['Your personal circumstances','The date you passed the test','The region of the UK you live in','The test centre you attended']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The form and the supporting evidence depend on each applicant''s own situation.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, from October 2013 people applying for settlement had to pass the Life in the UK test and also provide:',array['a letter from their employer','proof that they own a home','a certificate in British history','evidence of their English at B1 level']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): From October 2013 the test had to be combined with evidence of English at level B1 on the Common European Framework of Reference.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under the rules described for October 2013, which English skills had to be evidenced at B1 level?',array['Writing and spelling','Reading and writing','Speaking and listening','Reading and translation']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The new requirement was for evidence of English speaking and listening skills at B1.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.2-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The handbook says level B1 of the Common European Framework of Reference is equivalent to:',array['ESOL Level 2','ESOL Entry Level 1','ESOL Entry Level 3','level A2 of the same framework']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): B1 on the European framework is described as matching ESOL Entry Level 3.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, what has no place in British society?',array['Political disagreement','Religious belief','Regional accents','Extremism or intolerance']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The handbook states that extremism and intolerance have no place in British society.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, British values are protected by:',array['political parties and trade unions','royal decrees and Church rules','law, customs and expectations','international treaties alone']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The handbook says these principles come from the UK''s history and traditions, and that law, custom and expectation protect them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where are the UK''s fundamental values reflected, according to the handbook?',array['In the rules of the Church of England','Only in laws passed since 1945','In the responsibilities, rights and privileges of citizens and residents','Only in the words of the national anthem']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The values show in the responsibilities, rights and privileges of British citizens and permanent residents.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Alongside democracy and the rule of law, which of these does the handbook list as a fundamental principle of British life?',array['Compulsory military service','Membership of the established Church','Participation in community life','Ownership of property']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Participation in community life is one of the five fundamental principles listed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is one of the freedoms the UK offers in return for residents'' responsibilities?',array['Freedom from unfair discrimination','Freedom from paying any tax','Freedom from all public charges','Freedom to choose which laws apply']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): In return for residents'' responsibilities, the UK offers several freedoms, including freedom from unfair discrimination.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Freedom of belief and religion is one of the freedoms the UK offers to people living here.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The freedoms offered by the UK include freedom of belief and religion.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the citizenship pledge, to what do new citizens give their loyalty?',array['Their country of birth','The United Kingdom','Their local council','The Commonwealth']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The pledge begins with a promise of loyalty to the United Kingdom.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the citizenship pledge, new citizens promise to fulfil their:',array['duties and obligations as British citizens','religious vows and church duties','party membership and campaigning','military training and reserve service']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The pledge ends with a promise to carry out one''s duties and obligations as a British citizen.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the citizenship pledge, new citizens promise to respect the UK''s:',array['churches and cathedrals','sports and pastimes','royal palaces and parks','rights and freedoms']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): New citizens pledge loyalty to the UK and to respect its rights and freedoms.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.3-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who does the handbook say should respect and support the fundamental values and principles of British society?',array['All those living in the UK','Only British citizens','Only people in public office','Only people born in the UK']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The handbook expects everyone who lives in the UK to respect and support these values and principles.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many questions are there in the Life in the UK test?',array['40','30','24','20']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The test is made up of 24 questions on important aspects of life in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Everyone taking the Life in the UK test at the same session answers the same 24 questions.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Each person at a test session gets a different set of 24 questions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, apart from English, in which TWO languages can special arrangements be made to take the test?',array['Welsh and Scottish Gaelic','Scottish Gaelic and Cornish','Welsh and Irish Gaelic','Irish Gaelic and Cornish']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The test is normally in English, but arrangements can be made to sit it in Welsh or Scottish Gaelic.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, why should you take the test only at a registered test centre?',array['Other centres charge much higher fees','Only certificates from registered centres are accepted','Other centres only test in Welsh','Other centres use harder questions']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The UK Border Agency accepts certificates only from registered test centres.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, which people have separate arrangements for sitting the Life in the UK test?',array['Residents of London','Residents of the Isle of Man or the Channel Islands','Residents of Scotland or Wales','Residents aged over 60']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Residents of the Isle of Man and the Channel Islands have separate arrangements.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What must you take with you to the Life in the UK test?',array['A copy of the handbook','Identification and proof of your address','Two character references','A letter from your employer']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Candidates have to bring identification and proof of address to the test.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: If you arrive at the test without identification and proof of address, you will still be allowed to sit it.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Without identification and proof of address you cannot take the test.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does the handbook say about its ''Check that you understand'' boxes?',array['They are the only parts that are tested','They are guidance, and knowing them alone is not enough to pass','They contain all the test questions','They can be skipped because they are not tested']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): The boxes help identify key points, but you must understand the whole book to pass.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where in the handbook are some key words and phrases explained?',array['In the glossary at the back','In the ''Check that you understand'' boxes','In an appendix of test answers','In the index at the front']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): A glossary at the back of the handbook lists helpful key words and phrases.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, which website explains ESOL courses and where to find one near you?',array['Gov.uk','The Life in the UK test website','The UK Border Agency website','The National Trust website']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Gov.uk is named as the place to find out about ESOL courses in your area.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S1.4-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Test questions can be based on which parts of the handbook?',array['All parts, including the introduction','Everything except the introduction','Only the history chapter','Only the chapters on government and law']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 1 (The values and principles of the UK): Questions may come from any part of the book, the introduction included.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_values']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S2.2-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the status of the rest of Ireland, the part that is not in the UK?',array['It is an independent country','It is governed from Westminster','It is a Crown dependency','It is a British overseas territory']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Northern Ireland is part of the UK, while the rest of Ireland is an independent country.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S2.2-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the handbook, the words ''Britain'' and ''British'' are used to refer to:',array['only people born in the UK','only people in Great Britain','everyone in the UK','only people in England']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The handbook uses ''Britain'' and ''British'' to mean everyone in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S2.2-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where does the parliament that governs the UK sit?',array['Cardiff','Edinburgh','Westminster','Windsor']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): The UK is governed by the parliament at Westminster.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S2.2-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which parts of the UK have their own parliament or assembly with devolved powers?',array['England and Northern Ireland only','Scotland only','Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland','England, Scotland and Wales']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland each have their own parliament or assembly with powers in defined areas.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S2.2-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: British overseas territories such as St Helena are part of the UK.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 2 (What is the UK?): Overseas territories are linked to the UK but are not part of it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_what_is_uk']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which report described the welfare state that Clement Attlee''s new government promised to introduce?',array['The Butler Report','The Beveridge Report','The Keynes Report','The Macmillan Report']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Attlee became Prime Minister in 1945 promising to bring in the welfare state set out by William Beveridge.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the National Health Service, set up in 1948, guarantee?',array['Free hospital treatment only for children under 16','Free health care only for former soldiers and their families','A minimum standard of health care for everyone, free when used','Private health insurance paid for by every employer']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The NHS promised everyone a basic level of health care with no charge at the point of use.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The national system of benefits introduced after 1945 was meant to protect people ''from the cradle to the grave''.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A national ''social security'' benefits system was created so that people would be looked after throughout their lives.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO were taken into public ownership by the Labour government after 1945?',array['The coal mines and the newspapers','The supermarkets and the railways','The banks and the airlines','The railways and the coal mines']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The post-war Labour government nationalised the railways, coal mines and the gas, water and electricity supplies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which alliance did the UK join after the Second World War to resist the perceived threat from the Soviet Union?',array['NATO','The Commonwealth','The League of Nations','The Warsaw Pact']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The UK became a member of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, formed to resist a possible Soviet invasion.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Second World War the UK relied entirely on the United States and never developed its own atomic bomb.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook says the UK built its own atomic bomb in the post-war years and also joined NATO.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which party was in government in Britain from 1951 to 1964?',array['The Liberal Party','The Social Democratic Party','The Labour Party','The Conservative Party']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Britain had Conservative governments between 1951 and 1964, a time of recovery and rising prosperity.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which Prime Minister made the well-known ''wind of change'' speech on independence for colonies?',array['Winston Churchill','Clement Attlee','Harold Wilson','Harold Macmillan']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Harold Macmillan, Conservative Prime Minister, spoke of a ''wind of change'' bringing independence to countries of the Empire.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which profession did Clement Attlee give up so that he could do social work in East London?',array['Teacher','Barrister','Doctor','Journalist']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After Oxford University Attlee became a barrister, but he left the law for social work in East London before becoming a Labour MP.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What position did Clement Attlee hold in Winston Churchill''s wartime coalition government?',array['Deputy Prime Minister','Foreign Secretary','Minister for Health','Chancellor of the Exchequer']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Attlee served as Churchill''s deputy during the war and became Prime Minister when Labour won in 1945.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the official title of the 1942 Beveridge Report?',array['The Welfare of the British People','Social Insurance and Allied Services','A National Plan for Full Employment','Health and Housing for the Nation']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The report commonly called the Beveridge Report was formally titled Social Insurance and Allied Services.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which division in the school system, enforced by the Education Act 1944, still exists in most of Britain?',array['Between day schools and boarding schools','Between primary and secondary schools','Between boys'' schools and girls'' schools','Between church schools and state schools']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Although education has changed a great deal since 1944, the split between primary and secondary schools introduced by the Butler Act largely remains.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of work is Dylan Thomas''s Under Milk Wood?',array['A novel','A radio play','A collection of short stories','An opera']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Under Milk Wood is a radio play by Dylan Thomas; it was first performed in 1954, after his death.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For whom did Dylan Thomas write the poem Do Not Go Gentle into That Good Night?',array['His late wife','His dying father','His elderly mother','His young son']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Dylan Thomas wrote the poem in 1952 for his father, who was dying.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which city, where Dylan Thomas was born, has a statue of him and a centre named after him?',array['Newport','Cardiff','Swansea','Bangor']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Swansea, Dylan Thomas''s birthplace, remembers him with several memorials, including a statue and the Dylan Thomas Centre.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Facing labour shortages after 1945, the government encouraged workers from where to help rebuild Britain?',array['South Africa and Rhodesia','Ireland and other parts of Europe','Australia and New Zealand','Canada and the United States']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): To help with post-war reconstruction, the government encouraged workers from Ireland and elsewhere in Europe to come to the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year were people from the West Indies invited to come to work in Britain?',array['1948','1968','1958','1938']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1948 West Indians were also invited to come and work to help with post-war labour shortages.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Textile and engineering firms from which areas sent agents to India and Pakistan to recruit workers?',array['Wales and the south-west of England','London and the south-east of England','Scotland and Northern Ireland','The north of England and the Midlands']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In the 1950s firms in the north of England and the Midlands sent recruiters to India and Pakistan to find workers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By what name is the 1960s often known in Britain?',array['The Swinging Sixties','The Silent Sixties','The Golden Sixties','The Roaring Sixties']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The 1960s, a decade of great social change, was nicknamed ''the Swinging Sixties''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Beatles and The Rolling Stones were two well-known pop groups of the 1960s.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The 1960s saw British fashion, cinema and pop music flourish, with The Beatles and The Rolling Stones among the best-known groups.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 1960s, laws on divorce and on which other matter were liberalised in England, Wales and Scotland?',array['Immigration','Abortion','Military service','Hunting']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Social laws were relaxed in the 1960s, including those on divorce and on abortion in England, Wales and Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which right did Parliament give women during the social changes of the 1960s?',array['The right to vote at 21','The right to sit in the House of Lords','The right to equal pay','The right to stand for Parliament']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Parliament passed laws giving women equal pay and banning employers from discriminating against them because of their gender.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the 1960s many employers expected women to give up work once they married.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook notes that women were often expected to leave work on marriage, before new laws improved their position.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which supersonic passenger airliner did Britain develop together with France?',array['The Spitfire','The Harrier','The Hurricane','Concorde']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Britain and France jointly produced Concorde, the world''s only supersonic commercial airliner.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did the number of migrants arriving from the West Indies and South Asia fall in the late 1960s?',array['Economic growth kept workers at home','All vacancies in Britain had been filled','The Commonwealth was abolished','New laws restricted immigration to Britain']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The government passed new laws in the late 1960s limiting immigration, so the numbers arriving fell.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under the immigration laws of the late 1960s, what were immigrants required to have?',array['The ability to speak both English and Welsh','A university degree from a British institution','A job offer approved by Parliament','Close ties to Britain by birth or ancestry']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After the late 1960s, people wishing to settle needed close ties to Britain by birth or ancestry.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the early 1970s, 28,000 people of Indian descent expelled from which country were allowed into Britain?',array['South Africa','Uganda','India','Kenya']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Despite tighter immigration rules, Britain took in 28,000 people of Indian origin expelled from Uganda in the early 1970s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Between which two cities did John Logie Baird make the first television broadcast in 1932?',array['Glasgow and Edinburgh','London and Glasgow','London and Manchester','London and Edinburgh']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Scotsman John Logie Baird developed television in the 1920s and in 1932 broadcast between London and Glasgow.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the first successful radar test take place?',array['1935','1945','1915','1925']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Radar, developed by Sir Robert Watson-Watt, was first tested successfully in 1935.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where is the radio telescope built by Sir Bernard Lovell, for many years the largest in the world?',array['Jodrell Bank in Cheshire','Armagh in Northern Ireland','St Andrews in Fife','Greenwich in London']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Lovell''s work with radar led to discoveries in astronomy; his radio telescope at Jodrell Bank in Cheshire still operates.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Scottish physician John MacLeod was a co-discoverer of what?',array['Anaesthetics','Penicillin','The structure of DNA','Insulin']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): John MacLeod helped discover insulin, which is used to treat diabetes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was the structure of the DNA molecule discovered through work at British universities?',array['1978','1945','1923','1953']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Research in London and Cambridge revealed the structure of DNA in 1953.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which British scientist was among those awarded the Nobel Prize for discovering the structure of DNA?',array['John MacLeod','Francis Crick','Alan Turing','Bernard Lovell']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Francis Crick, a Briton, shared the Nobel Prize for the discovery of DNA''s structure.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Concorde begin carrying passengers?',array['1969','1976','1959','1986']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Concorde first flew in 1969, entered passenger service in 1976 and was retired in 2003.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was special about the Harrier jump jet, designed and developed in the UK?',array['It could take off vertically','It was the first jet airliner','It could take off and land on water','It carried passengers faster than sound']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Harrier jump jet was a British-designed aircraft able to take off vertically.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who invented the cash-dispensing ATM, or ''cashpoint'', in the 1960s?',array['Robert Edwards','Peter Mansfield','Christopher Cockerell','James Goodfellow']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): James Goodfellow invented the ATM in the 1960s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO people pioneered IVF therapy in Britain?',array['Francis Crick and John MacLeod','Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe','Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell','Peter Mansfield and Bernard Lovell']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): IVF treatment for infertility was pioneered by physiologist Sir Robert Edwards and gynaecologist Patrick Steptoe.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which town was the world''s first ''test-tube baby'' born in 1978?',array['Grantham','Oldham','Swansea','Enfield']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The first baby conceived by IVF was born in Oldham, Lancashire, in 1978.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Dolly the sheep was cloned in 1996 by a team led by Sir Ian Wilmot and which other British scientist?',array['Peter Mansfield','Patrick Steptoe','Keith Campbell','Francis Crick']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell led the team that first cloned a mammal, Dolly the sheep, in 1996.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Sir Peter Mansfield was co-inventor of which medical device?',array['The X-ray machine','The ultrasound scanner','The MRI scanner','The heart pacemaker']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Peter Mansfield co-invented magnetic resonance imaging, which gives detailed, non-invasive images of internal organs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did the post-war economic boom in Britain come to an end?',array['In the early 1990s','In the early 1950s','In the late 1950s','In the late 1970s']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook says the post-war boom ended in the late 1970s as prices rose sharply and the pound was unstable.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were the ''balance of payments'' problems of the 1970s?',array['Wages rose much faster than prices','Imports were worth more than the money earned from exports','Exports were worth far more than imports','The pound was permanently fixed to the dollar']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Rising prices and an unstable pound meant Britain was paying more for imports than it received for its exports.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the 1970s many people argued that the trade unions had too little power.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Frequent strikes in the 1970s led many to argue that unions had become too powerful and were damaging the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What happened to the Northern Ireland Parliament in 1972?',array['It merged with the Irish Parliament','It gained new powers over taxation','It was suspended and the UK government ruled directly','It moved from Belfast to Londonderry']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Because of serious unrest, the Northern Ireland Parliament was suspended in 1972 and direct rule from the UK government followed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','About how many people lost their lives in the violence in Northern Ireland in the decades after 1969?',array['Some 300','Some 300,000','Some 30,000','Some 3,000']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook puts the number killed in Northern Ireland''s violence since 1969 at around 3,000.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which event did Mary Peters win an Olympic gold medal in 1972?',array['The marathon','The long jump','The pentathlon','The 100 metres']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mary Peters, an athlete from Northern Ireland, won Olympic gold in the pentathlon in 1972.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The athlete Mary Peters was born in Belfast.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mary Peters was born in Manchester and moved to Northern Ireland as a child.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Margaret Thatcher''s father was a grocer in which town?',array['Oldham, Lancashire','Enfield, north London','Grantham, Lincolnshire','Swansea, Wales']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Margaret Thatcher grew up in Grantham in Lincolnshire, where her father was a grocer.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did Margaret Thatcher train as before her political career?',array['A doctor and a nurse','A chemist and a lawyer','An economist and a banker','A teacher and a journalist']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Thatcher trained in chemistry and law before being elected a Conservative MP in 1959.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was Margaret Thatcher elected Leader of the Conservative Party?',array['1970','1959','1975','1979']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Thatcher became Conservative leader, and so Leader of the Opposition, in 1975; she became Prime Minister in 1979.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: No Prime Minister of the 20th century served longer than Margaret Thatcher.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Thatcher held office from 1979 to 1990, longer than any other 20th-century Prime Minister.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which United States President did Margaret Thatcher work closely with?',array['Franklin Roosevelt','Dwight Eisenhower','John F Kennedy','Ronald Reagan']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): As Prime Minister, Thatcher worked closely with President Ronald Reagan.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Margaret Thatcher was one of the first Western leaders to welcome changes in the leadership of which country, helping to end the Cold War?',array['The Soviet Union','China','Argentina','East Germany']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Thatcher was quick to recognise new Soviet leadership, changes which ultimately helped bring the Cold War to a close.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did the Thatcher government make structural changes to the economy?',array['By fixing the prices of all goods','By abolishing income tax completely','By privatising nationalised industries','By nationalising the railways and mines']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Conservative government of 1979-90 sold off nationalised industries and placed legal limits on trade union powers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Conservative government led by Margaret Thatcher gave trade unions greater legal powers.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Thatcher''s government did the opposite: it introduced legal controls on what trade unions could do.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What effect did deregulation in the 1980s have on the City of London?',array['It was placed under direct government control','It became the centre of British coal mining','Its role as an international financial centre grew greatly','It lost most of its banks to other countries']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Deregulation greatly increased the City''s importance as a world centre for investment, insurance and financial services.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO traditional industries declined during the 1980s?',array['Financial services and insurance','Coal mining and insurance','Shipbuilding and coal mining','Shipbuilding and financial services']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): While the City grew, traditional industries like shipbuilding and coal mining declined under Thatcher.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Roald Dahl was born in Wales to parents of which nationality?',array['Norwegian','Dutch','Swedish','Danish']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Roald Dahl was born in Wales; his parents were Norwegian.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is one of Roald Dahl''s best-known books?',array['Under Milk Wood','Charlie and the Chocolate Factory','Jude the Obscure','Treasure Island']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Dahl is best known for children''s books such as Charlie and the Chocolate Factory and George''s Marvellous Medicine.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who led the Labour Party to victory in the 1997 general election?',array['Gordon Brown','Harold Wilson','Tony Blair','John Major']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Labour won in 1997 under Tony Blair, whose government created devolved bodies in Scotland and Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO devolved bodies did the Blair government introduce?',array['A Cornish Assembly and a London Parliament','A Welsh Assembly and an English Assembly','An English Parliament and a Scottish Parliament','A Scottish Parliament and a Welsh Assembly']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Labour government elected in 1997 set up a Scottish Parliament and a Welsh Assembly.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, how did the Welsh Assembly''s powers compare with those of the Scottish Parliament when the two were created?',array['It had fewer law-making powers but considerable control of public services','It had greater law-making powers than the Scottish Parliament','It had exactly the same powers as the Scottish Parliament','It had law-making powers but no say over public services']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Scottish Parliament was given substantial powers to make laws, while the Welsh Assembly had fewer but controlled many public services.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was the Northern Ireland Assembly first elected?',array['1972','2007','1990','1999']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Assembly was first elected in 1999, the year after the Good Friday Agreement was signed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-064','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Northern Ireland Assembly was suspended in 2002. When was it reinstated?',array['2010','2003','2014','2007']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After being suspended in 2002, the Assembly was not restored until 2007.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who became Prime Minister after Tony Blair in 2007?',array['Gordon Brown','David Cameron','John Major','Theresa May']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Gordon Brown took over from Tony Blair as Prime Minister in 2007.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After an Iraqi invasion in 1990, coalition forces with a leading British role liberated which country?',array['Afghanistan','Iran','Kuwait','Saudi Arabia']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In the 1990s Britain played a leading part in the coalition that freed Kuwait after Iraq invaded it in 1990.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: British forces took a leading part in coalition action in the former Yugoslavia during the 1990s.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): As well as Kuwait, British forces took a leading part in the coalition involved in the former Yugoslavia.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did British combat troops leave Iraq?',array['2014','2003','2007','2009']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): British armed forces took part in operations in Iraq after 2000, and combat troops withdrew in 2009.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-069','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, the UN-mandated International Security Assistance Force (ISAF) in Afghanistan was a coalition of about how many nations?',array['50','100','25','10']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook says the UK served in Afghanistan within the 50-nation ISAF coalition, at the Afghan government''s invitation.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-070','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The general election of May 2010 was the first since when in which no party won an overall majority?',array['1997','1979','February 1974','1945']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 2010 no single party won a majority, something that had last happened in the UK in February 1974.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.2-071','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO parties formed the coalition government in 2010?',array['The Conservatives and Labour','Labour and the Scottish National Party','The Conservatives and the Liberal Democrats','Labour and the Liberal Democrats']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After the 2010 election the Conservatives and Liberal Democrats formed a coalition, with David Cameron as Prime Minister.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was read at the coronation of William and Mary?',array['The Magna Carta','The Habeas Corpus Act','A Declaration of Rights','The Act of Union']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A Declaration of Rights read at the coronation stopped the king from raising taxes or administering justice without Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Under the Bill of Rights of 1689, the king or queen had to be a Protestant.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Parliament decided who could be monarch and insisted that the king or queen must be Protestant.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under the Bill of Rights, how often did a new Parliament originally have to be elected?',array['At least every seven years','At least every three years','At least every year','At least every ten years']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Elections had to be held at least every three years; this later became seven and is now five.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Today, what is the longest gap allowed between elections for a new Parliament?',array['Seven years','Three years','Four years','Five years']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The original three-year limit became seven years and is now five years.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After 1689, what did the monarch have to ask Parliament to renew every year?',array['Funding for the army and the navy','Permission to appoint bishops','Licences for newspapers','Approval of the royal marriage']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Each year the monarch needed Parliament''s agreement to keep funding the army and navy.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By what name is the modern Conservative Party still sometimes known?',array['The Whigs','The Tories','The Chartists','The Roundheads']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Whigs and Tories were the two main groups in Parliament, and Conservatives are still sometimes called Tories.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The laws passed after the Glorious Revolution mark the beginning of which system?',array['Constitutional monarchy','Absolute monarchy','Republican government','Universal suffrage']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): These laws began constitutional monarchy: the monarch stayed important but could no longer force policies on Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the decades after William III, women who owned property were allowed to vote.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Only men owning property of a certain value could vote; no women had the vote at all.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were constituencies controlled by a single wealthy family called?',array['Rotten boroughs','Crofts','Pocket boroughs','Plantations']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A constituency in the control of one rich family was called a pocket borough.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were constituencies with hardly any voters called?',array['Clans','Rotten boroughs','Plantations','Pocket boroughs']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Constituencies with very few voters were known as rotten boroughs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Jewish people settle in London for the first time since the Middle Ages?',array['1870','1656','1707','1066']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1656 Jewish people settled in London, the first to arrive since the Middle Ages.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did the Huguenots leave France?',array['They wanted to work in British factories','They had supported James II','They were escaping a potato famine','They were persecuted for their Protestant religion']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Huguenots were French Protestants who fled religious persecution between 1680 and 1720.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO kinds of work did many Huguenots do in Britain?',array['Farming and fishing','Railway and canal building','Mining and shipbuilding','Banking and weaving']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many Huguenots were educated and skilled, working as scientists, in banking, or in weaving and other crafts.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why was there uncertainty about who would succeed Queen Anne?',array['She had been forced to abdicate','She had no surviving children','She had converted to Catholicism','Parliament had abolished the monarchy']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Queen Anne left no surviving children, which made the succession uncertain and led to the 1707 union.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the Act of Union of 1707 called in Scotland?',array['The Declaration of Rights','The Act for the Government of Scotland','The Bill of Rights','The Treaty of Union']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In Scotland the 1707 agreement that formed the Kingdom of Great Britain is known as the Treaty of Union.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which church did Scotland keep after the union of 1707?',array['The Church of England','The Methodist Church','The Roman Catholic Church','Its Presbyterian Church']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Scotland lost its independence but kept its own Presbyterian Church, as well as its legal and education systems.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Parliament choose George I to be king in 1714?',array['He had won the Battle of Culloden','He was Anne''s eldest son','He was Anne''s nearest Protestant relative','He was the son of James II']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Parliament picked the German George I because he was Queen Anne''s closest Protestant relative.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 1714, who tried to make James II''s son king instead of George I?',array['Irish Fenians','English Puritans','French Huguenots','Scottish Jacobites']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): An attempt by Scottish Jacobites to crown James II''s son was quickly defeated.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did George I need to rely on his ministers?',array['He was a prisoner of Parliament','He did not speak very good English','He was too young to rule alone','He lived in America']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): George I''s weak English made him depend more on his ministers, leading to the role of Prime Minister.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','During which years was Sir Robert Walpole Prime Minister?',array['1801 to 1815','1721 to 1742','1745 to 1760','1689 to 1702']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Walpole, the first man called Prime Minister, held the post from 1721 to 1742.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 1745, Bonnie Prince Charlie tried to replace which king?',array['George I','George II','James II','William III']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The 1745 rising aimed to put a Stuart back on the throne in place of George II, George I''s son.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How was Bonnie Prince Charlie related to James II?',array['He was his grandson','He was his nephew','He was his son','He was his brother']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles Edward Stuart, known as Bonnie Prince Charlie, was James II''s grandson.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After Culloden, what did many clansmen become?',array['Members of the House of Lords','Officers in George II''s army','Landlords of large estates','Tenants who paid for the land they used']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The clans lost power after Culloden; chieftains favoured by the king became landlords and clansmen became paying tenants.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were ''crofts'' in the Scottish Highlands?',array['Large flocks of sheep','Clan chieftains'' castles','Highland churches','Small individual farms']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): During the Highland Clearances, landlords destroyed many crofts (small farms) to make room for sheep and cattle.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did evictions during the Highland Clearances become very common?',array['The 12th century','The early 19th century','The early 16th century','The late 20th century']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Evictions from the land became very common in the early 1800s, and many Scots emigrated to North America.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which languages did Robert Burns write?',array['Welsh and English','Gaelic only','Latin and French','Scots, Scottish-flavoured English and standard English']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Burns wrote in the Scots language, in English mixed with Scottish words, and in standard English.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: As well as writing his own poems, Robert Burns changed or added lyrics to many traditional folk songs.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Burns revised a large number of traditional folk songs by altering or adding lyrics.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On what occasion is Auld Lang Syne usually sung?',array['New Year celebrations','The State Opening of Parliament','Remembrance Day','Easter Sunday']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Burns'' song Auld Lang Syne is sung in the UK and elsewhere to celebrate the New Year, called Hogmanay in Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which century did the Enlightenment take place?',array['The 18th century','The 16th century','The 14th century','The 19th century']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Enlightenment was the development of new ideas in politics, philosophy and science during the 1700s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Many of the great thinkers of the Enlightenment came from which country?',array['Ireland','Wales','Scotland','The Netherlands']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many leading Enlightenment thinkers, such as Adam Smith and David Hume, were Scottish.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which Enlightenment principle is still important in the UK today?',array['Only property owners should have the vote','Everyone may hold their own political and religious beliefs','The king rules by the will of God alone','The state should choose people''s religion']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A key Enlightenment idea was that people may hold their own political and religious beliefs without the state dictating them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Before the 18th century, what was the biggest source of employment in Britain?',array['Shipbuilding','Banking','Coal mining','Agriculture']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Farming employed more people than anything else in Britain before the 1700s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were cottage industries?',array['Factories built in country villages','Companies building houses for workers','Farms growing wheat for export','People working from home to make goods such as cloth and lace']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Before industrialisation many people produced goods like cloth and lace in their own homes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Britain was the first country in the world to industrialise on a large scale.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Through the Industrial Revolution, Britain became the first country to industrialise widely.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO developments made the Industrial Revolution possible?',array['Electricity and computers','The jet engine and television','Machinery and steam power','Nuclear power and radar']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Industrial Revolution happened because of new machinery and the use of steam power.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO industries developed because of the Bessemer process?',array['Weaving and lace-making','Banking and insurance','Farming and fishing','Shipbuilding and railways']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mass-produced steel from the Bessemer process led to the growth of shipbuilding and the railways.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did Richard Arkwright originally train and work as?',array['A blacksmith','A barber','A sailor','A coal miner']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Arkwright began as a barber who dyed hair and made wigs before moving into textiles.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which machine did Richard Arkwright improve?',array['The carding machine','The spinning jenny','The steam locomotive','The printing press']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Arkwright improved the original carding machine and developed horse-driven spinning mills.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is carding?',array['Dyeing finished cloth','Preparing fibres for spinning into yarn and fabric','Cutting coal underground','Printing patterns onto cloth']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Carding prepares fibres so that they can be spun into yarn and made into fabric.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For what is Richard Arkwright particularly remembered?',array['Abolishing child labour','Inventing the railway engine','Building the Great Western Railway','Running his factories efficiently and profitably']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Arkwright is best remembered for how efficiently and profitably he ran his factories.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: During the Industrial Revolution, laws protected employees from long hours and dangerous work.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): There were no laws protecting workers, who often worked long hours in dangerous conditions; children were treated the same as adults.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who mapped the coast of Australia?',array['Captain James Cook','Admiral Nelson','Sir Walter Raleigh','Sir Francis Drake']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Captain James Cook mapped Australia''s coast, and a few colonies were then set up there.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the East India Company originally set up to do?',array['Govern Australia','Fight France','Trade','Build railways']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The East India Company was created for trade but came to control large parts of India.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO goods did Britain import from North America and the West Indies?',array['Sugar and tobacco','Textiles and tea','Coal and iron','Tea and spices']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Britain imported sugar and tobacco from North America and the West Indies, and textiles, tea and spices from India and Indonesia.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which region of India did Sake Dean Mahomet grow up?',array['Bengal','Kashmir','Punjab','Goa']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mahomet, born in 1759, grew up in Bengal and later served in the Bengal army.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which London street did Sake Dean Mahomet open his coffee house?',array['Baker Street','George Street','Oxford Street','Downing Street']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mahomet opened the Hindoostane Coffee House, Britain''s first curry house, in George Street in 1810.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the 18th century, slavery was legal within Britain itself.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Slavery was illegal inside Britain, although the overseas slave trade was a fully established industry.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','From which region did most slaves transported on British ships come?',array['West Africa','East Africa','India','South America']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Slaves came mainly from West Africa and were taken to America and the Caribbean.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which kinds of plantation were slaves made to work in America and the Caribbean?',array['Rubber and spices','Potato and wheat','Tobacco and sugar','Tea and coffee']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Enslaved people were forced to work on tobacco and sugar plantations.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were abolitionists?',array['Campaigners for votes for women','People who opposed the monarchy','People who supported ending slavery','Traders who bought and sold slaves']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Abolitionists, including William Wilberforce, wanted slavery abolished and turned public opinion against the trade.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did the Royal Navy act against the slave trade?',array['It ignored the trade at sea','It protected British slave ships','It stopped other countries'' slave ships and freed the slaves','It carried slaves to the Caribbean']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Royal Navy intercepted foreign slave ships, freed the slaves and punished the traders.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why had many colonist families originally gone to North America?',array['To escape the Irish famine','To work in coal mines','To fight against France','To have religious freedom']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many colonists'' families had moved to North America for religious freedom, and they valued ideas of liberty.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which slogan did American colonists use against British taxes?',array['Votes for women','Liberty, equality and fraternity','No taxation without representation','God save the king']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The colonists saw British taxes as an attack on their freedom and demanded representation in Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Britain recognise the independence of the American colonies?',array['1805','1783','1776','1789']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After the colonists defeated the British army, Britain accepted their independence in 1783.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was there a revolution in France?',array['1776','1789','1805','1815']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): France had a revolution in 1789, after which its new government quickly went to war with Britain.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Admiral Nelson survived the Battle of Trafalgar and later became Prime Minister.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Nelson led the British fleet at Trafalgar and died during the battle; it was Wellington who later became Prime Minister.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where is Nelson''s Column?',array['Edinburgh Castle','Portsmouth harbour','Trafalgar Square, London','Hyde Park, London']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Nelson''s Column in Trafalgar Square is a monument to Admiral Nelson.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where can Nelson''s ship, HMS Victory, be visited?',array['Bristol','Liverpool','Portsmouth','Plymouth']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): HMS Victory, Nelson''s flagship, is in Portsmouth.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the Duke of Wellington''s nickname?',array['The Iron Duke','The Bard','The Lord Protector','The Black Prince']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Wellington, who defeated Napoleon at Waterloo, was known as the Iron Duke.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After his military career, the Duke of Wellington became Prime Minister.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Iron Duke went on to serve as Prime Minister.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Ireland became unified with England, Scotland and Wales in 1801 after which law?',array['The Act of Union of 1707','The Act of Union of 1800','The Bill of Rights of 1689','The Reform Act of 1832']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Act of Union of 1800 joined Ireland with Great Britain from 1801.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the country created in 1801 called?',array['The Commonwealth of England','The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland','The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland','The Kingdom of Great Britain']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Union with Ireland in 1801 formed the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How is the cross of St George described?',array['A diagonal white cross on a blue ground','A white cross on a red ground','A diagonal red cross on a white ground','A red cross on a white ground']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): England''s patron saint, St George, is represented by a red cross on a white ground.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-064','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was the first Union Flag created from the flags of Scotland and England?',array['1606','1603','1707','1801']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The first Union Flag was made in 1606, when Wales was already united with England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Until which year did Queen Victoria reign?',array['1901','1888','1837','1914']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Victoria reigned from 1837 until 1901, almost 64 years.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, which monarch had reigned longer than any other British monarch at the time of writing?',array['James I','Henry VIII','Elizabeth I','Queen Victoria']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook, written in 2013, describes Victoria''s reign of almost 64 years as the longest of any British monarch.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which group became increasingly significant within the UK during the Victorian Age?',array['The Scottish clans','The middle classes','The Huguenots','The landed aristocracy']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In the Victorian Age the middle classes grew in importance and reformers worked to improve life for the poor.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Roughly how many British citizens left the UK between 1853 and 1913?',array['About 1 million','About 120,000','As many as 13 million','About 400 million']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many people were encouraged to settle overseas, and up to 13 million left between 1853 and 1913.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-069','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Between 1870 and 1914, which group of around 120,000 people came to Britain to escape persecution?',array['Russian and Polish Jews','Dutch Boers','French Huguenots','Indian and Chinese workers']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): About 120,000 Russian and Polish Jews arrived in this period, many settling in London''s East End, Manchester and Leeds.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-070','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year were the Corn Laws repealed?',array['1867','1815','1846','1832']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Repealing the Corn Laws in 1846 was an example of the government''s free-trade policies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-071','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What had the Corn Laws prevented?',array['The export of British coal','Farmers from growing wheat','The import of cheap grain','The building of canals']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Corn Laws blocked cheap grain imports; their repeal helped industry by making raw materials cheaper to import.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-072','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 1847, what was the legal limit on the daily working hours of women and children?',array['14 hours','10 hours','8 hours','12 hours']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A law of 1847 restricted women and children to working 10 hours per day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-073','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which father and son pioneered the railway engine?',array['George and Robert Stephenson','Richard and John Arkwright','Isambard and Marc Brunel','James and Thomas Watt']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Just before Victoria''s reign, George and Robert Stephenson pioneered the railway engine.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-074','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where was Isambard Kingdom Brunel originally from?',array['Glasgow','Liverpool','Bristol','Portsmouth']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The engineer Brunel came from Portsmouth in England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-075','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At which London station does Brunel''s Great Western Railway start?',array['Euston','Waterloo','King''s Cross','Paddington']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Great Western Railway, Britain''s first major railway, runs from Paddington to the south west, the West Midlands and Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-076','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the 19th century, less than a tenth of all the iron, coal and cotton cloth made in the world came from the UK.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): British industry led the world: over half of all the iron, coal and cotton cloth produced globally came from the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-077','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides manufacturing, the UK became a world centre for which services in the 19th century?',array['Insurance and banking','Tourism and travel','Computing and software','Film and television']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The UK became a centre for financial services such as insurance and banking.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-078','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where was the Great Exhibition of 1851 held?',array['In Buckingham Palace','In the Tower of London','In the Crystal Palace in Hyde Park','In St Paul''s Cathedral']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Great Exhibition opened in 1851 in the Crystal Palace, a vast steel-and-glass building in Hyde Park.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-079','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Most of the objects shown at the Great Exhibition came from countries outside Britain.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Countries from around the world exhibited, but most of the objects were British-made.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-080','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the Crimean War, Britain fought alongside Turkey and France against which country?',array['The Netherlands','Spain','Russia','Germany']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): From 1853 to 1856 Britain, Turkey and France fought Russia in the Crimean War.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-081','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Crimean War was the first war to be what?',array['Fought with tanks','Fought in southern Africa','Extensively covered by the media','Fought using aircraft']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): News stories and photographs made the Crimean War the first war widely reported in the media.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-082','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the Crimean War, many soldiers died from illnesses caught in the hospitals rather than from their wounds.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Conditions were very poor, and disease picked up in hospitals killed many soldiers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-083','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which medal did Queen Victoria introduce during the Crimean War?',array['The George Cross','The Iron Cross','The Military Cross','The Victoria Cross']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Victoria introduced the Victoria Cross, which honours acts of valour by soldiers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-084','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which country was Florence Nightingale born?',array['Turkey','Italy','Germany','England']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Florence Nightingale''s parents were English, but she was born in Italy.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-085','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did Florence Nightingale train as a nurse?',array['Turkey','France','Germany','Italy']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): At 31 she trained as a nurse in Germany, then in 1854 went to military hospitals in Turkey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-086','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Florence Nightingale set up her training school for nurses?',array['1854','1870','1851','1860']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): She founded the Nightingale Training School at St Thomas'' Hospital in 1860; it still exists.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-087','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Florence Nightingale is often regarded as the founder of what?',array['The National Health Service','Modern nursing','The Red Cross','The Royal Society']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Because of her work and her training school, Nightingale is often seen as the founder of modern nursing.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-088','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 19th-century Ireland, what proportion of the population still depended on farming?',array['One-quarter','One-tenth','One-half','Two-thirds']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Two-thirds of Irish people still made their living from farming, often on very small plots.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-089','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','About how many people died in the Irish famine of the mid-19th century?',array['A hundred thousand','Five million','Thirteen million','A million']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): When the potato crop failed, a million people died of disease and starvation and another million and a half emigrated.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-090','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which Irish Nationalists favoured complete independence for Ireland?',array['The Jacobites','The Chartists','The Fenians','The Whigs']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Within the 19th-century Irish Nationalist movement, the Fenians wanted full independence.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-091','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who campaigned for ''Home Rule'' for Ireland?',array['Oliver Cromwell','William Wilberforce','Charles Stuart Parnell','Robert Walpole']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles Stuart Parnell argued for Home Rule rather than full independence.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-092','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did ''Home Rule'' mean for Ireland?',array['Joining with France','Staying in the UK but with its own parliament','Becoming fully independent of the UK','Being ruled from London with no Irish MPs']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Under Home Rule Ireland would remain part of the UK while having its own parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-093','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Reform Act of 1832 abolished the old pocket and rotten boroughs.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The 1832 Act ended the pocket and rotten boroughs and gave more seats to towns and cities.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-094','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Reform Act of 1832, members of the working class gained the right to vote.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Voting still depended on owning property, so working-class people still could not vote.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-095','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which year''s Reform Act created many more urban seats and lowered the property needed to vote?',array['1832','1846','1867','1918']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Following the Chartists'' campaign, the Reform Act of 1867 added urban seats and reduced the property qualification.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-096','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Reform Act of 1867, most men were able to vote.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Even after 1867 most men still had no vote, and no women could vote.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-097','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is universal suffrage?',array['The right of all men only to vote','The right of anyone to stand for Parliament','The right of every adult man and woman to vote','The right of property owners to vote']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Universal suffrage means every adult man and woman has the vote; it came in the 20th century.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-098','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which years were Acts passed letting married women hold on to their own earnings and property?',array['1870 and 1882','1807 and 1833','1918 and 1928','1832 and 1867']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Acts passed in 1870 and 1882 allowed married women to retain their earnings and property.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-099','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were women who campaigned for the vote in the late 19th and early 20th centuries known as?',array['Chartists','Puritans','Suffragettes','Abolitionists']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Women campaigning for greater rights, especially the vote, formed the women''s suffrage movement and were called suffragettes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-100','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which organisation did Emmeline Pankhurst set up in 1889?',array['The Women''s Institute','The Women''s Franchise League','The Women''s Social and Political Union','The Women''s Land Army']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1889 Pankhurst founded the Women''s Franchise League to win local election votes for married women; the WSPU followed in 1903.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-101','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO forms of protest did the suffragettes use?',array['Burning ballot papers and leaving the country','Occupying factories and blocking railways','Chaining themselves to railings and hunger strikes','Refusing to pay rent and boycotting tea']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The WSPU used civil disobedience, including chaining themselves to railings, smashing windows, arson and hunger strikes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-102','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 1918, what right did women over 30 gain in addition to the vote?',array['The right to join the army','The right to own property','The right to stand for Parliament','The right to keep their own earnings']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): From 1918 women over 30 could both vote and stand for Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-103','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Women over 30 were given the vote in 1918 partly in recognition of what?',array['Their support for the Boer War','Their part in the Chartist petitions','Their contribution to the First World War effort','Their work at the Great Exhibition']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Votes for women over 30 in 1918 partly recognised what women had done for the war effort.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-104','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: At the end of the 19th century, the great majority of British people believed the Empire was a force for good.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Despite debate about the Empire''s future, most British people saw it as a force for good in the world.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-105','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who were the Boers?',array['Settlers in Ulster from Scotland','Protestant refugees from France','Settlers in South Africa from the Netherlands','Nationalists who wanted Irish independence']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Boer War of 1899-1902 was fought in South Africa against settlers of Dutch origin.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-106','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which country was Rudyard Kipling born?',array['South Africa','The USA','Scotland','India']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Born in India in 1865, Kipling went on to live in India, the UK and the USA.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-107','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was Rudyard Kipling awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature?',array['1865','1936','1907','1918']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Kipling won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1907.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.3-108','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these books by Rudyard Kipling is still popular today?',array['Oliver Twist','Treasure Island','The Jungle Book','Robinson Crusoe']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Kipling''s books, including the Just So Stories and The Jungle Book, remain popular.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO social measures were introduced in Britain in the early 20th century?',array['The National Health Service and free school meals','Old-age pensions and free school meals','A national minimum wage and child benefit','Free university tuition and old-age pensions']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Reforms in the early 1900s included pensions for the elderly, free meals for schoolchildren and financial help for people out of work.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which early 20th-century reform helped more people to enter public life?',array['MPs were paid a salary for the first time','The House of Lords was abolished','The voting age was lowered to 18','Women were allowed to become judges']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Introducing a salary for MPs meant people without private wealth could more easily enter Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the early 20th century town planning rules were tightened to stop more slums being built.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Among the reforms of the period were stricter planning rules to prevent new slums, and laws to make workplaces safer.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Whose assassination on 28 June 1914 triggered the events that led to the First World War?',array['King George V of Britain','Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria','Tsar Nicholas II of Russia','Kaiser Wilhelm II of Germany']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The killing of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in June 1914 triggered the First World War.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, which of these helped create the conditions for the First World War?',array['A worldwide economic depression','Growing nationalism in many European states','A dispute over the Falkland Islands','The rise of Adolf Hitler']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Besides the assassination, nationalism, militarism, imperialism and the split of Europe''s powers into two camps set the scene for war.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO countries fought alongside Britain as Allied Powers in the First World War?',array['Russia and the Ottoman Empire','Germany and France','Bulgaria and Japan','France and Russia']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Britain''s allies included France, Russia, Japan, Belgium and Serbia, later joined by others including the United States.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Approximately how many Indians fought on behalf of Britain in the First World War?',array['About 40,000','More than a million','About 100,000','About 10,000']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Over a million Indians fought for Britain in many countries, and about 40,000 of them were killed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which countries were the main Central Powers in the First World War?',array['France, Russia, Belgium and Serbia','Germany, Austria-Hungary, Russia and Greece','Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire and Bulgaria','Germany, Italy, Japan and Bulgaria']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Allies'' main enemies were Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire, with Bulgaria joining them later.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: British casualties in the First World War numbered more than 2 million.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Millions were killed or wounded in the war, including over 2 million British casualties.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the British government promise Ireland in 1913?',array['Home Rule','Full independence as a republic','Partition into two states','Direct rule from London']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1913 Ireland was promised Home Rule: its own parliament while remaining in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What name is given to the 1916 uprising against the British in Dublin?',array['The Easter Rising','The Jacobite Rebellion','The Dublin Spring','The Glorious Revolution']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Irish Nationalists, unwilling to wait until after the First World War, rose against British rule in Dublin in 1916.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The leaders of the 1916 Easter Rising were pardoned and released by the British.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The leaders of the Easter Rising were executed under military law, and a guerrilla war followed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the name of the southern part of Ireland after 1922?',array['The Irish Free State','The Irish Dominion','The Kingdom of Ireland','The Southern Province']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The mainly Protestant six northern counties stayed in the UK, while the rest became the Irish Free State.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the Irish Free State become a republic?',array['1949','1937','1973','1922']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Irish Free State, which governed itself, became a republic in 1949.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the conflict between those wanting full Irish independence and those loyal to the British government often called?',array['The Blitz','The Troubles','The Plantations','The Clearances']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Disagreement over the partition of Ireland led to a terror campaign, and the conflict is often called ''the Troubles''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the world enter the ''Great Depression''?',array['1929','1933','1939','1919']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Great Depression began in 1929 and caused mass unemployment in some parts of the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which new industries developed in the UK during the depression of the 1930s?',array['The automobile and aviation industries','The textile and wool industries','The shipbuilding and coal industries','The computing and electronics industries']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Traditional heavy industries such as shipbuilding suffered, but car and aircraft manufacturing grew.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did car ownership in the UK change between 1930 and 1939?',array['It tripled from 1 million to 3 million','It halved from 2 million to 1 million','It doubled from 1 million to 2 million','It rose from 100,000 to 1 million']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): As prices fell, people in work had more to spend, and the number of cars rose from one to two million.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO writers were prominent in Britain between the two world wars?',array['Charles Dickens and Jane Austen','Graham Greene and Evelyn Waugh','Graham Greene and Geoffrey Chaucer','Evelyn Waugh and William Shakespeare']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The inter-war years were a time of cultural blossoming, with Graham Greene and Evelyn Waugh among the leading writers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which economist published influential new theories of economics between the wars?',array['Adam Smith','Richard Arkwright','Robert Walpole','John Maynard Keynes']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): John Maynard Keynes published important new economic theories in the inter-war period.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the BBC begin in 1936?',array['Its very first radio broadcasts','The world''s first regular television service','The first transatlantic telephone service','Britain''s first commercial radio station']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1936 the BBC launched the first regular television service anywhere in the world.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Adolf Hitler come to power in Germany?',array['1929','1933','1918','1939']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Hitler took power in 1933, believing the post-1918 terms imposed on Germany were unfair.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which event in 1939 led Britain and France to declare war on Germany?',array['The invasion of the Soviet Union','Hitler''s invasion of Poland','The occupation of Austria','The bombing of Pearl Harbour']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Britain had hoped to avoid another war, but Hitler''s attack on Poland in 1939 led Britain and France to declare war on Germany.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which countries were the Axis powers at the start of the Second World War?',array['Germany, Austria and Turkey','Germany, the Soviet Union and Japan','Germany, Spain and Bulgaria','Germany, Italy and Japan']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): At first the Allies faced the Axis: fascist Germany and Italy, together with Imperial Japan.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Canada, Australia and New Zealand were among the main countries on the allied side at the start of the Second World War.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Besides the UK, France and Poland, the leading Allies included Canada, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO countries did Hitler take control of after invading Poland and before advancing through France?',array['Belgium and the Netherlands','Britain and Ireland','Spain and Portugal','Austria and Czechoslovakia']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Hitler had already occupied Austria and invaded Czechoslovakia; after Poland he seized Belgium and the Netherlands, then moved through France in 1940.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who became Prime Minister and Britain''s war leader in 1940?',array['Rab Butler','Winston Churchill','Clement Attlee','Harold Macmillan']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): As German forces swept through France in 1940, Winston Churchill became Prime Minister and led Britain in the war.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which phrase arose from the 1940 evacuation of troops from France?',array['''The Blitz spirit''','''The wind of change''','''The cradle to the grave''','''The Dunkirk spirit''']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Although much was lost, the successful rescue from Dunkirk gave rise to the expression ''the Dunkirk spirit''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Spitfire and the Hurricane, the RAF''s key planes in the Battle of Britain, were designed and built in the United States.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Spitfire and the Hurricane were British-designed and British-built aircraft.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which city was almost totally destroyed by German bombing during the Blitz?',array['Glasgow','Cardiff','Coventry','Manchester']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Night-time bombing of British cities in the Blitz almost completely destroyed Coventry and badly damaged London''s East End.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which phrase is still used today when Britons unite to face hardship together?',array['''The Swinging Sixties''','''The Dunkirk evacuation''','''The Great Depression''','''The Blitz spirit''']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Despite the bombing, a strong national spirit of resistance grew, remembered today as ''the Blitz spirit''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Churchill lost the general election of 1945. In which year did he return as Prime Minister?',array['1951','1955','1950','1964']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After defeat in 1945, Churchill became Prime Minister again in 1951.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Winston Churchill was given a state funeral after his death in 1965.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Churchill remained an MP until 1964 and was honoured with a state funeral when he died in 1965.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Churchill''s words ''Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few'' were spoken during what?',array['The D-Day landings','Victory in Europe in 1945','The Dunkirk evacuation','The Battle of Britain']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Churchill made this speech to the House of Commons in 1940, during the Battle of Britain.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Churchill''s ''We shall fight on the beaches'' speech to the House of Commons followed which event?',array['The D-Day landings','The fall of Singapore','The evacuation from Dunkirk','The attack on Pearl Harbour']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Churchill gave the famous ''fight on the beaches'' speech to the Commons after Dunkirk in 1940.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did the Japanese defeat the British before going on to occupy Burma and threaten India?',array['Singapore','Egypt','Australia','India']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): While defending Britain, British forces also fought elsewhere; in Singapore they were defeated by the Japanese, who then occupied Burma.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What brought the United States into the Second World War?',array['The fall of France in 1940','Japan''s bombing of Pearl Harbour in December 1941','The German bombing of London in 1940','Germany''s invasion of Poland in 1939']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The USA joined the war after Japan bombed the American naval base at Pearl Harbour in December 1941.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 1941 Hitler launched history''s largest invasion against which country?',array['Britain','The Soviet Union','France','Poland']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Germany invaded the Soviet Union in 1941; its forces were eventually driven back with huge losses, a turning point in the war.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Allies won significant victories in North Africa and Italy during the Second World War.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Allies gradually gained the advantage, with important victories in North Africa and Italy.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did the Allies comprehensively defeat Germany?',array['June 1944','May 1945','August 1945','November 1918']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After D-Day the Allies advanced through France into Germany, which was defeated in May 1945.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What ended the war against Japan in August 1945?',array['A peace treaty signed in Singapore','A British invasion of the Japanese islands','US atom bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki','The capture of Tokyo by Soviet troops']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The war with Japan ended when the United States used atom bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which scientist led the team that was the first to ''split the atom''?',array['Ernest Rutherford','Francis Crick','Alexander Fleming','Alan Turing']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Scientists led by Ernest Rutherford were the first to split the atom.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Manhattan Project, which developed the atomic bomb, was based in Britain.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Manhattan Project was in the United States; British scientists from Rutherford''s team took part in it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was Alexander Fleming researching in 1928 when he discovered penicillin?',array['Diabetes','Malaria','Influenza','Tuberculosis']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928 while he was studying the flu.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which scientists turned penicillin into a usable drug?',array['Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe','Howard Florey and Ernst Chain','Francis Crick and John MacLeod','Ian Wilmot and Keith Campbell']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Fleming''s discovery was developed into a usable medicine by Florey and Chain, and it was mass-produced by the 1940s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.4-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Penicillin is used today to treat viral infections.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Penicillin is still used to treat bacterial infections, not viral ones.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which period of history ran from the Norman Conquest until about 1485?',array['The Bronze Age','The Middle Ages','The Iron Age','The Tudor period']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The years after the Norman Conquest up to around 1485 are known as the Middle Ages.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is another name for the Middle Ages?',array['The medieval period','The Reformation','The Enlightenment','The Restoration']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Middle Ages are also called the medieval period.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Middle Ages were a time of almost constant war.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The medieval period saw nearly continuous warfare at home and abroad.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which law of 1284 annexed Wales to the Crown of England?',array['The Magna Carta','The Act of Union','The Statute of Rhuddlan','The Act for the Government of Wales']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Edward I''s Statute of Rhuddlan of 1284 joined Wales to the English Crown.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Edward I introduce the Statute of Rhuddlan?',array['1314','1215','1348','1284']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Edward I annexed Wales to the Crown of England with the Statute of Rhuddlan in 1284.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By when had the last Welsh rebellions against English rule been defeated?',array['The start of the 18th century','The middle of the 15th century','The end of the 11th century','The middle of the 13th century']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The final Welsh rebellions were crushed by the mid-1400s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the last Welsh rebellions were defeated, what was introduced in Wales?',array['Scottish laws and Gaelic','Norman French as the official language','English laws and the English language','A separate Welsh parliament']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Once the rebellions ended, English law and the English language were brought into Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the outcome of the Battle of Bannockburn?',array['The Scots beat the English and Scotland stayed unconquered','Scotland and England were united under one king','The Vikings were driven out of Scotland','The English conquered all of Scotland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): At Bannockburn in 1314 the Scots defeated the English, and Scotland was never conquered by England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was Ireland''s status at the beginning of the Middle Ages?',array['A province of the Roman Empire','Part of the Kingdom of England','An independent country','Ruled by the Scottish king']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Ireland was independent when the Middle Ages began.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did the English first go to Ireland in the Middle Ages?',array['As settlers escaping the Black Death','As merchants buying wool','As troops to help the Irish king','As missionaries spreading Christianity']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): English soldiers first went to Ireland to help the Irish king and then stayed to found settlements.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the Middle Ages, some important lords in parts of Ireland outside the Pale accepted the authority of the English king.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Besides ruling the Pale, the English king was accepted by some major lords elsewhere in Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were the Crusades?',array['Wars fought by European Christians to control the Holy Land','Wars between England and France over French land','Civil wars between the Houses of Lancaster and York','Campaigns to annex Wales to England']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many English knights took part in the Crusades, fought by European Christians for the Holy Land.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: At the Battle of Agincourt, Henry V''s English army greatly outnumbered the French.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry V''s army was heavily outnumbered at Agincourt in 1415 but still defeated the French.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which decade did the English leave France?',array['The 1550s','The 1450s','The 1650s','The 1350s']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After the long wars with France, the English left France in the 1450s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the system of land ownership used by the Normans called?',array['Feudalism','Common law','The plantation system','The clan system']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Normans held land under the feudal system.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under feudalism, what did the king receive in return for giving land to his lords?',array['Payment in gold coins','A share of the harvest','Help in war','New churches and abbeys']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The king granted land to his lords so that they would support him in war.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under the feudal system, what did landowners have to provide?',array['Men to serve in the army','Stone for building castles','Teachers for local schools','Ships for the navy']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Landowners were obliged to send a set number of men to fight in the army.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the Middle Ages, what were most peasants?',array['Knights','Clergy','Gentry','Serfs']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A few peasants had their own land, but most of them were serfs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these was true of medieval serfs?',array['They owned large areas of land','They were paid high wages by the king','They elected members of the House of Commons','They worked for their lord and were not free to leave']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Serfs grew food on a small patch of their lord''s land, worked for him and were not free to leave.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The feudal system of land ownership also developed in southern Scotland.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The same feudal system grew up in the south of Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the Middle Ages, who owned the land in the north of Scotland and in Ireland?',array['The bishops','Members of the clans','Norman barons','The English king']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In northern Scotland and Ireland land belonged to members of prominent families known as clans.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the Black Death probably a form of?',array['Smallpox','Plague','Cholera','Influenza']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The disease that arrived in 1348 was probably a type of plague.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Black Death killed a much smaller share of the population in Scotland and Wales than in England.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): About a third of England''s people died, and a similar share in Scotland and Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the Black Death, why was there less need to grow cereal crops?',array['More grain was imported','The climate became colder','Most people ate fish instead','The population was smaller']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): With far fewer people alive, less cereal was needed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did peasants begin to demand after the Black Death?',array['Their own parliament','Higher wages','The right to vote','Free land from the king']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Labour shortages after the Black Death led peasants to ask for more pay.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the Black Death, owners of large areas of land formed a new class later known as the:',array['clergy','clans','gentry','serfs']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Large landowners became a new social class, later called the gentry.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What developed in the towns after the Black Death as a result of growing wealth?',array['A strong middle class','The first trade unions','A new royal court','Large Viking settlements']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Growing wealth in the towns produced a strong middle class.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did the Black Death affect the English-controlled area of Ireland?',array['It grew to cover all of Ireland','It was given back to the Irish king','It became smaller for a time','It was not affected at all']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many people in the Pale died, so for a while the area under English control shrank.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The origins of Parliament can be traced to which body?',array['The Scottish clans','The Roman senate','The town guilds','The king''s council of advisers']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Parliament grew out of the council of advisers that served the king.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO groups were members of the medieval king''s council of advisers?',array['Merchants and serfs','Important noblemen and Church leaders','Merchants and craft workers','Important noblemen and serfs']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The king''s council included leading noblemen and the heads of the Church.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which king was forced by his noblemen to agree to the Magna Carta?',array['Edward I','Richard III','King John','Henry V']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1215 King John''s noblemen made him accept the Magna Carta.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does the name ''Magna Carta'' mean?',array['The King''s Law','The Great Charter','The Royal Book','The Great Council']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Magna Carta is Latin for the Great Charter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Magna Carta restricted the king''s power to do what?',array['Collect taxes or make and change laws','Appoint bishops and abbots','Build castles in Wales','Choose his own heir']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The charter protected the nobles'' rights and limited the king''s power over taxes and laws.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Before 1215, there were few formal limits on the power of the king.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Until the Magna Carta of 1215, the king''s power had few formal limits.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the Magna Carta, whom would the king need to involve in his decisions?',array['The Scottish king','The Pope','The peasants','His noblemen']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): From then on the king had to include his noblemen in decision-making.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In medieval England, when in particular did the king call a parliament?',array['Every spring after Easter','When a new bishop was chosen','When he needed to raise money','When a battle had been lost']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Parliaments were summoned so the king could consult his nobles, especially to raise money.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who sat in the medieval House of Lords?',array['The nobility, great landowners and bishops','Judges and lawyers','Knights and wealthy townspeople','Peasants chosen by their villages']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The House of Lords was made up of nobles, great landowners and bishops.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who were elected to sit in the medieval House of Commons?',array['The king''s sons and nephews','Bishops and abbots','Knights and wealthy people from towns and cities','Serfs and peasants']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Knights, usually smaller landowners, and rich townspeople were elected to the Commons.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In the Middle Ages, most of the population could vote in elections to the House of Commons.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Only a small part of the population could take part in electing members of the Commons.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were the three Houses of the medieval Scottish Parliament called?',array['Councils','Chambers','Estates','Assemblies']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Scotland''s Parliament had three Houses, known as Estates.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides the lords, which TWO groups made up the Estates of the medieval Scottish Parliament?',array['The knights and the judges','The commons and the clergy','The clergy and the judges','The commons and the knights']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The three Estates were the lords, the commons and the clergy.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which legal principle began to be established in the Middle Ages?',array['That the king chooses every jury','That judges are independent of the government','That judges are elected by the people','That only bishops may judge cases']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The idea of judicial independence from government started to take hold in this period.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did the legal system in medieval Scotland differ from that in England?',array['Scottish laws were codified, or written down','Scotland had no courts at all','Scottish judges followed previous decisions','Scottish laws were made by the clergy alone']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): English judges built common law from precedent, while in Scotland laws were written down.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the Norman Conquest, which language did the king and his noblemen speak?',array['Anglo-Saxon','Gaelic','Norman French','Latin']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Norman king and nobles spoke Norman French while peasants kept speaking Anglo-Saxon.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO modern English words are based on Norman French words?',array['Apple and cow','Park and summer','Park and beauty','Cow and beauty']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): ''Park'' and ''beauty'' come from Norman French; ''apple'', ''cow'' and ''summer'' come from Anglo-Saxon.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these English words is based on an Anglo-Saxon word?',array['Demand','Summer','Park','Beauty']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): ''Summer'' is one of the handbook''s examples of words with Anglo-Saxon origins.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','''Demand'' comes from French. Which word with a similar meaning does the handbook say comes from Anglo-Saxon?',array['Require','Request','Ask','Insist']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook pairs French ''demand'' with Anglo-Saxon ''ask'' as words of similar meaning.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By about which year were official documents in England being written in English?',array['1066','1400','1600','1215']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): By 1400 official documents in England were written in English.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: By 1400, Norman French was still the preferred language of the English royal court and Parliament.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): By 1400 English, not French, was the preferred language at the royal court and in Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What are the characters in The Canterbury Tales doing?',array['Building a great cathedral','Sailing to the Holy Land','Going on a pilgrimage and telling stories','Fighting in the Hundred Years War']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Chaucer''s poems describe pilgrims travelling to Canterbury and the tales they tell each other on the way.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which language did Geoffrey Chaucer write The Canterbury Tales?',array['Scots','Norman French','English','Latin']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Chaucer wrote his series of poems in English.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did Geoffrey Chaucer write The Canterbury Tales?',array['In the years leading up to 1400','Just after the Norman Conquest','In the 17th century','During the reign of Henry VIII']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Chaucer wrote the poems in the period before 1400.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Canterbury Tales was one of the first books printed by William Caxton.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Caxton, who first printed books on a press in England, printed The Canterbury Tales among his earliest titles.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In medieval Scotland, besides Gaelic, which language developed and was used by a number of poets?',array['Norman French','Welsh','Scots','Latin']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many Scots kept speaking Gaelic, and the Scots language also developed and was used by poets.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who wrote The Bruce, a poem about the Battle of Bannockburn?',array['Geoffrey Chaucer','John Barbour','William Caxton','Robert Burns']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): John Barbour, writing in Scots, composed The Bruce about Bannockburn.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which medieval castle does the handbook mention as still being in use today?',array['Maiden Castle','Housesteads','Windsor Castle','Sutton Hoo']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many medieval castles are ruins, but some, such as Windsor and Edinburgh, are still used.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why were castles built in many places in medieval Britain and Ireland?',array['To store wool for export','Partly for defence','As places to print books','As hospitals during the Black Death']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Castles were built across Britain and Ireland, partly to defend the land.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the stained glass windows in medieval cathedrals tell stories about?',array['The lives of English kings','The Bible and Christian saints','Famous battles against France','Viking legends']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Several cathedrals had stained glass windows showing stories from the Bible and of Christian saints.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','York Minster is famous for its:',array['Roman mosaics','stained glass','printing press','wooden roof']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The handbook gives the glass in York Minster as a famous example of medieval stained glass.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which product became a very important English export in the Middle Ages?',array['Coal','Wool','Cotton','Tea']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): England was a major trading nation and English wool was a very important export.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Skilled weavers who came to work in medieval England came from which country?',array['Holland','Germany','Italy','France']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): People with special skills came to England, including weavers from France.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','From which country did canal builders come to work in medieval England?',array['Italy','France','Germany','Holland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Canal builders from Holland were among the skilled workers who came to England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','From which country did glass manufacturers come to work in medieval England?',array['Germany','France','Italy','Holland']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Italian glass manufacturers were among the skilled workers who came to England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-064','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the Wars of the Roses begin?',array['1415','1455','1348','1485']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The civil war known as the Wars of the Roses started in 1455.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the Wars of the Roses fought to decide?',array['Who should be king of England','Who should control Wales','Who owned the land in Ireland','Whether England should leave France']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Wars of the Roses was a civil war over who should be king of England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who led the House of Lancaster and became king after the Battle of Bosworth Field?',array['Edward I','Henry V','Henry Tudor','Richard of York']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry Tudor, leader of the Lancastrians, became King Henry VII after Bosworth.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Whom did Henry VII marry, uniting the Houses of Lancaster and York?',array['Catherine of Aragon','Jane Seymour','Elizabeth of York','Anne of Cleves']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VII married Elizabeth of York, King Richard''s niece, joining the two families.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How was Elizabeth of York related to King Richard III?',array['She was his sister','She was his niece','She was his wife','She was his daughter']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VII''s wife, Elizabeth of York, was Richard III''s niece.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.5-069','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who became the first Tudor king?',array['Richard III','Edward VI','Henry VIII','Henry VII']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VII, formerly Henry Tudor, was the first Tudor king.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Henry VII was careful with money. What did this allow him to do?',array['Fund Drake''s voyage around the world','Pay for a long war against Spain','Build the first Tower of London','Build up the monarchy''s financial reserves']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VII was thrifty, so he was able to build up the Crown''s financial reserves.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Henry VIII reversed his father''s policy and handed more power back to the nobles.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VIII carried on his father''s policy of centralising power rather than reversing it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many times did Henry VIII marry?',array['Five','Eight','Six','Four']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VIII is famous for having six wives as well as for breaking with the Church of Rome.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which child of Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon survived?',array['Anne','Edward','Mary','Elizabeth']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry and Catherine of Aragon had several children, but Mary was the only one to survive.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where was Anne Boleyn executed?',array['The Tower of London','Westminster Hall','Hampton Court Palace','Windsor Castle']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Anne Boleyn, accused of taking lovers, was put to death at the Tower of London.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Jane Seymour died shortly after giving birth to Henry VIII''s son.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Jane Seymour gave Henry his longed-for son, Edward, but died soon after the birth.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which wife of Henry VIII was a German princess, married for political reasons?',array['Jane Seymour','Catherine of Aragon','Catherine Howard','Anne of Cleves']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Anne of Cleves came from Germany; Henry married her for political reasons and soon divorced her.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In Henry VIII''s new Church of England, who had the power to appoint bishops?',array['The bishops themselves','The Pope','The king','Parliament']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In the Church of England the king, and no longer the Pope, appointed bishops and decided how people should worship.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the Reformation?',array['A movement against the Pope''s authority and Catholic practices','A campaign to give all adult men the vote','A movement to restore Catholicism in England','A plan to unite England and Scotland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Reformation spread across Europe as a challenge to the Pope''s authority and to Roman Catholic ideas and practices.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Instead of Latin, in which language did Protestants read the Bible?',array['Hebrew','Aramaic','Greek','Their own languages']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Protestants read the Bible in their own languages rather than in Latin.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Protestants at the time of the Reformation prayed to saints and at shrines.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Protestants rejected praying to saints and at shrines.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What led to rebellion by Irish chieftains in the Tudor period?',array['The repeal of the Corn Laws in Ireland','The failure of the Irish potato crop','English attempts to impose Protestantism and English land laws','The creation of the Union Flag']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): English efforts to force Protestantism on Ireland, together with English rules on inheriting land, provoked rebellion and brutal fighting.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After Wales was formally united with England under Henry VIII, where did the Welsh send representatives?',array['The Pope in Rome','The House of Commons','The Scottish Parliament','The Lord Lieutenant in Dublin']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Under the Act for the Government of Wales, Welsh representatives sat in the House of Commons and Welsh law was reformed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which son of Henry VIII was strongly Protestant?',array['Edward VI','James II','Henry VII','Charles I']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VIII''s successor was his son Edward VI, a firm Protestant.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How old was Edward VI when he died?',array['9','18','21','15']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Edward VI died aged 15, having reigned for just over six years.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Elizabeth I questioned people closely about their real religious beliefs.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Elizabeth required church attendance and set rules for services, but she did not enquire into what people really believed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How did Elizabeth I avoid serious religious conflict in England?',array['By making Catholicism the official religion','By letting people stop attending church','By balancing Catholic and more extreme Protestant views','By abolishing the Church of England']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Elizabeth found a middle way between Catholics and the more extreme Protestants, which kept religious peace in England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Spain send the Armada against England in 1588?',array['To claim Ireland for the Spanish crown','To conquer England and restore Catholicism','To protect Spanish trade with India','To support Parliament against the king']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Armada was a large Spanish fleet sent to conquer England and bring back Catholicism; its defeat made Elizabeth very popular.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the Scottish Parliament abolish the Pope''s authority in Scotland?',array['1588','1534','1603','1560']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In 1560 the mainly Protestant Scottish Parliament ended papal authority and made Roman Catholic services illegal.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Unlike the Church of England, the Protestant Church of Scotland established in the 16th century was not a state Church.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A Protestant Church of Scotland was set up, but unlike in England it was not a state Church.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How old was Mary Stuart when she became Queen of Scotland?',array['Eighteen','Six years old','A week old','Fifteen']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mary''s father died when she was only a week old, making her queen as a baby.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Mary, Queen of Scots flee to England?',array['She had been defeated by Cromwell','She had lost the Battle of Culloden','She was suspected of involvement in her husband''s murder','Scotland had been invaded by Spain']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After her husband was murdered and suspicion fell on her, Mary escaped to England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','To whom did Mary, Queen of Scots give her throne?',array['Her Protestant son, James VI','Her husband''s family','Her Catholic brother, Charles','Her cousin, Elizabeth I']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mary handed the Scottish throne to her son James VI, who was a Protestant.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How was Mary, Queen of Scots related to Elizabeth I?',array['She was her sister','She was her aunt','She was her daughter','She was her cousin']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Mary, Elizabeth I''s cousin, hoped Elizabeth would help her, but Elizabeth distrusted her.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For how long did Elizabeth I keep Mary, Queen of Scots a prisoner?',array['11 years','40 years','20 years','5 years']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Suspecting that Mary wanted the English throne, Elizabeth held her captive for 20 years.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which commander in the defeat of the Spanish Armada is seen as a founding figure of English naval tradition?',array['Captain James Cook','Sir Francis Drake','The Duke of Wellington','Admiral Nelson']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Sir Francis Drake helped defeat the Armada and is regarded as a founding figure of English naval tradition.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Golden Hind was one of the first ships to do what?',array['Defeat the French at Trafalgar','Cross the Atlantic using steam','Reach the coast of Australia','Sail right around the world']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Drake''s Golden Hind was among the first ships to circumnavigate the globe.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In Elizabeth I''s time, where did English settlers first begin to establish colonies?',array['The eastern coast of America','The coast of Australia','India','Southern Africa']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): English settlers began colonising America''s east coast during Elizabeth I''s reign.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides writing plays and poems, what else was William Shakespeare?',array['A judge','A sea captain','A bishop','An actor']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Shakespeare was both a playwright and an actor, as well as a poet.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are among Shakespeare''s most famous plays?',array['Macbeth and The Canterbury Tales','A Midsummer Night''s Dream and Macbeth','Romeo and Juliet and The Jungle Book','Hamlet and Paradise Lost']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Among Shakespeare''s best-known plays are Hamlet, Macbeth, Romeo and Juliet and A Midsummer Night''s Dream.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Shakespeare was one of the first writers to portray which people?',array['Only kings and queens','Ordinary English men and women','Settlers in the American colonies','Workers in steam-powered factories']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Although he wrote about historical events, Shakespeare did not limit himself to royalty and was an early portrayer of ordinary English people.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Shakespeare invented many words that are still in common use today.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Shakespeare greatly influenced the English language and created many words still used now.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','''Once more unto the breach'' is a line from which Shakespeare play?',array['Hamlet','Macbeth','Henry V','As You Like It']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): This often-quoted line comes from Shakespeare''s Henry V.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which Shakespeare play does the line ''A rose by any other name'' appear?',array['Hamlet','A Midsummer Night''s Dream','Romeo and Juliet','Henry V']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): ''A rose by any other name'' is a quotation from Romeo and Juliet.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','''All the world''s a stage'' comes from which Shakespeare play?',array['Henry V','Hamlet','Romeo and Juliet','As You Like It']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The line ''All the world''s a stage'' is from As You Like It.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The phrase ''The darling buds of May'' comes from which work by Shakespeare?',array['Henry V','Sonnet 18','Macbeth','Hamlet']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The phrase comes from Shakespeare''s Sonnet 18.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the Globe Theatre in London?',array['The original theatre Shakespeare built, still unchanged','The building where the Great Exhibition of 1851 was held','A modern copy of the kind of theatre where Shakespeare''s plays were first staged','A theatre designed by Sir Christopher Wren after 1666']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Today''s Globe Theatre is a modern reconstruction of the theatres that first staged Shakespeare''s plays.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Elizabeth I married and had children, but none of them outlived her.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Elizabeth I never married and had no children, so her cousin James VI of Scotland inherited the throne.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By what other name is the King James Version of the Bible known?',array['The Common Version','The Royal Version','The Authorised Version','The Geneva Bible']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The English Bible translated in King James''s reign is called the King James Version or the Authorised Version.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The King James Bible was the first Bible ever translated into English.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The King James Version was not the first English Bible, but it is still used in many Protestant churches.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What title did Henry VIII take in relation to Ireland?',array['Lord Lieutenant of Ireland','Duke of Ulster','Lord Protector of Ireland','King of Ireland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Henry VIII extended English authority over Ireland and called himself King of Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In Tudor Ireland, whose instructions were local leaders expected to follow?',array['The Pope in Rome','The Irish chieftains','The Scottish Parliament','The Lord Lieutenants in Dublin']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): English laws were brought in and local leaders had to obey the Lord Lieutenants based in Dublin.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Many of the new settlers in the Ulster plantations came from which area?',array['South-west Scotland','Cornwall','South Wales','Northern England']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many Ulster settlers came from south-west Scotland, while other land was handed to London-based companies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which monarch was very skilled at managing Parliament?',array['Charles I','Elizabeth I','James I','James II']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Elizabeth I balanced her own wishes against those of both Houses of Parliament, unlike James I and Charles I.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For how many years did Charles I raise money without Parliament''s approval?',array['3','7','11','20']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles I governed without Parliament for 11 years until trouble in Scotland forced him to recall it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Charles I''s revised Prayer Book cause serious unrest in Scotland?',array['It was printed only in Latin','He tried to impose it on the Presbyterian Church there','It made the Pope head of the Church','It banned the Bible in English']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles wanted more ceremony in worship and tried to force his revised Prayer Book on Scotland''s Presbyterian Church, provoking unrest.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Charles I recall Parliament to ask for funds?',array['1629','1642','1649','1640']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Needing money to fight a Scottish army, Charles recalled Parliament in 1640.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who were the Puritans?',array['Supporters of James II in exile','French Protestant refugees','Protestants who wanted strict and simple religious worship','Catholics who supported the Pope']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Puritans were Protestants who favoured strict, plain doctrine and worship; many sat in Parliament and opposed Charles I''s church reforms.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Roman Catholics in Ireland rebel against the government in the 1640s?',array['The Corn Laws had been introduced','Henry VIII had just taken the Irish crown','They feared the growing power of the Puritans','The potato crop had failed']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A rebellion broke out in Ireland because Catholics there were afraid of the rising influence of the Puritans.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many parliamentary leaders did Charles I try to arrest in the House of Commons?',array['Seven','Three','Five','Twelve']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles I entered the Commons to arrest five leaders, but the men had been warned and stayed away.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Several monarchs have entered the House of Commons since Charles I''s attempted arrests.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): No monarch has set foot in the House of Commons since Charles I tried to arrest the five members.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the English Civil War begin?',array['1640','1646','1649','1642']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): War between the king and Parliament could no longer be avoided and broke out in 1642.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were supporters of Parliament called in the English Civil War?',array['Roundheads','Tories','Cavaliers','Jacobites']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The country divided into the king''s supporters, the Cavaliers, and Parliament''s supporters, the Roundheads.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After the execution of Charles I, what was the English republic called?',array['The Kingdom of Great Britain','The United Kingdom','The Commonwealth','The Glorious Republic']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): England became a republic without a monarch, known as the Commonwealth.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Oliver Cromwell is still a controversial figure in Ireland because of the violence of his campaign there.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Cromwell imposed the English Parliament''s authority in Ireland so violently that he remains controversial there.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After Charles I''s execution, whom did the Scots declare to be king?',array['James VI','William of Orange','His son, Charles II','Oliver Cromwell']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Scots, who had opposed the execution, proclaimed Charles I''s son, Charles II, as their king.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At which TWO battles did Cromwell defeat the Scottish army led by Charles II?',array['The Boyne and Bannockburn','Marston Moor and Naseby','Culloden and Killiecrankie','Dunbar and Worcester']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles II, crowned in Scotland, led an army into England but was beaten by Cromwell at Dunbar and Worcester.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did Charles II famously hide after the Battle of Worcester?',array['In a church crypt','In a haystack','In an oak tree','In a sea cave']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles II escaped from Worcester, on one occasion hiding in an oak tree, and later fled to Europe.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Oliver Cromwell die?',array['1649','1658','1666','1660']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Cromwell ruled as Lord Protector until his death in 1658.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who became Lord Protector after Oliver Cromwell died?',array['Samuel Pepys','His son, Richard','Charles II','Isaac Newton']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Richard Cromwell succeeded his father but could not control the army or the government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','From which country did Charles II return from exile in 1660?',array['France','The Netherlands','Spain','Ireland']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In May 1660 Charles II was invited by Parliament to return from the Netherlands, where he had been in exile.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Restoration, Roman Catholics and Puritans alike were excluded from power.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Church of England became the established Church again, and Catholics and Puritans were shut out of power.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who wrote a diary describing the plague and the Great Fire of London?',array['Sir Christopher Wren','Sir Edmund Halley','Samuel Pepys','Sir Isaac Newton']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Samuel Pepys described these events in his diary, which was published later and is still read.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-064','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does ''habeas corpus'' mean?',array['Let the people decide','The king can do no wrong','You must present the person in court','No taxation without representation']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Habeas corpus is a Latin phrase meaning that the person must be brought before a court.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the Habeas Corpus Act become law?',array['1707','1679','1660','1689']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Habeas Corpus Act of 1679 ensured no one could be imprisoned unlawfully, and it still matters today.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which early member of the Royal Society correctly predicted the return of a comet?',array['Samuel Pepys','Sir Edmund Halley','Sir Isaac Newton','Sir Christopher Wren']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Sir Edmund Halley, an early Royal Society member, predicted the comet''s return; it now bears his name.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which county was Isaac Newton born?',array['Yorkshire','Lincolnshire','Cornwall','Kent']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Newton was born in Lincolnshire in eastern England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did Isaac Newton discover about white light?',array['It contains no colours at all','It is composed of the colours of the rainbow','It travels more slowly than sound','It is produced only by the Sun']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Newton showed that white light is composed of the colours of the rainbow.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-069','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Charles II die?',array['1688','1679','1685','1660']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Charles II died in 1685 without legitimate children, and his brother James succeeded him.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-070','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Under what title did James II rule in Scotland?',array['James I','James VII','James VI','James III']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): James was James II in England, Wales and Ireland but James VII in Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-071','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','James II went against an Act of Parliament by allowing Roman Catholics to do what?',array['Become army officers','Become Lord Protector','Own land in Ulster','Sit on the throne of Scotland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): James II favoured Catholics and let them serve as army officers, which Parliament had banned.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-072','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What made people fear that the monarch after James II would not be Protestant?',array['James''s daughters became Catholics','Parliament abolished the Church of England','William of Orange became a Catholic','James''s wife had a son']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): James''s heirs were two Protestant daughters until his wife gave birth to a son, who would take precedence.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-073','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','William of Orange was the Protestant ruler of which country?',array['The Netherlands','Scotland','France','Spain']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): William of Orange ruled the Netherlands and was married to James II''s elder daughter, Mary.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-074','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was William of Orange''s title as king of Scotland?',array['William II','William IV','William I','William III']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): In Scotland he was William II, while in England, Wales and Ireland he was William III.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-075','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Glorious Revolution, William ruled jointly with Mary.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): William took the throne and shared rule with his wife Mary.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-076','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','With whose help did James II invade Ireland in an attempt to regain the throne?',array['A French army','The Royal Navy','A Spanish fleet','A Dutch army']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): James II landed in Ireland backed by a French army but was beaten at the Boyne.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-077','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Battle of the Boyne is still celebrated by some people in Northern Ireland today.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): William''s 1690 victory at the Boyne is still celebrated by some in Northern Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-078','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After William re-conquered Ireland, what happened to Irish Catholics?',array['They were allowed to be army officers','They were given their own parliament','They could not take part in the government','They were granted Home Rule']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many restrictions were placed on the Catholic Church in Ireland and Catholics were excluded from government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-079','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where was an armed rebellion in support of James II quickly defeated in Scotland?',array['Bannockburn','Culloden','Dunbar','Killiecrankie']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A Scottish rising in support of James was soon defeated at Killiecrankie.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-080','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which clan was killed after being late to take the oath accepting William as king?',array['The Campbells of Argyll','The Frasers of Lovat','The Stuarts of Atholl','The MacDonalds of Glencoe']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): All clans had to swear to accept William; the MacDonalds of Glencoe were late and were massacred.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.6-081','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were the supporters of James II called?',array['Huguenots','Whigs','Jacobites','Roundheads']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): People who still saw James as the rightful king, especially in Scotland, were known as Jacobites.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For much of the Stone Age, how was Britain connected to the continent?',array['By a stone causeway','By a frozen river','By a chain of small islands','By a land bridge']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): During much of the Stone Age a land bridge joined Britain to the continent.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which animals did Stone Age hunter-gatherers follow as they came and went?',array['Herds of cattle and pigs','Herds of deer and horses','Flocks of sheep and goats','Packs of wolves and bears']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Hunter-gatherers moved with the herds of deer and horses they hunted.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','About how long ago did the first farmers arrive in Britain?',array['10,000 years ago','6,000 years ago','2,000 years ago','4,000 years ago']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Farming began in Britain roughly 6,000 years ago.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What does the handbook say Stonehenge was probably used for?',array['The burial place of an Anglo-Saxon king','A special gathering place for seasonal ceremonies','A defended hill fort','A Roman temple']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Stonehenge was probably a place where people gathered for seasonal ceremonies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Skara Brae has helped archaeologists understand how people lived near the end of which period?',array['The Roman period','The Bronze Age','The Stone Age','The Iron Age']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The well-preserved village of Skara Brae shows how people lived late in the Stone Age.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','About how long ago did people in Britain learn to make bronze?',array['10,000 years ago','4,000 years ago','6,000 years ago','2,000 years ago']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Bronze Age began around 4,000 years ago.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of homes did people live in during the Bronze Age?',array['Longships','Timber-framed townhouses','Stone castles','Roundhouses']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Bronze Age people lived in roundhouses.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were round barrows?',array['Tombs where Bronze Age people buried their dead','Defended hilltop sites of the Iron Age','Stone Age fishing boats','Roman storage buildings']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Bronze Age people buried their dead in tombs called round barrows.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Bronze Age metalworkers made beautiful objects from bronze and which other metal?',array['Iron','Gold','Silver','Tin']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Skilled Bronze Age metalworkers made tools, ornaments and weapons in bronze and gold.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which period followed the Bronze Age?',array['The Roman period','The Iron Age','The Middle Ages','The Stone Age']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After the Bronze Age came the Iron Age, when people learned to work iron.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were Iron Age hill forts?',array['Defended sites where people sometimes lived','Tombs for burying the dead','Places for seasonal ceremonies','Camps built by the Roman army']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Iron Age people lived in roundhouses, sometimes inside defended sites called hill forts.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the Iron Age, most people were:',array['traders, sailors or monks','farmers, craft workers or warriors','miners, builders or priests','hunter-gatherers or fishermen']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Most Iron Age people farmed, worked at crafts or were warriors.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Languages related to those spoken in the Iron Age are still spoken in parts of Wales, Scotland and Ireland.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Celtic languages related to Iron Age speech survive in parts of Wales, Scotland and Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What were some of the first coins minted in Britain inscribed with?',array['Pictures of Roman emperors','The names of Iron Age kings','Christian crosses','The year they were made']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Iron Age people made Britain''s first coins, some carrying the names of Iron Age kings.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, what marks the beginning of British history?',array['The arrival of the Romans','The first coins made in Britain, some bearing kings'' names','The building of Stonehenge','The arrival of the first farmers']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The first British coins, some with the names of Iron Age kings, are said to mark the start of British history.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','After Julius Caesar''s failed invasion, for about how long did Britain remain separate from the Roman Empire?',array['About 1,000 years','Nearly 100 years','Over 400 years','About 10 years']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After 55 BC Britain stayed outside the Roman Empire for almost a century, until AD 43.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where is there a statue of Boudicca?',array['On Westminster Bridge in London','Outside Edinburgh Castle','In Trafalgar Square','On Hadrian''s Wall']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): A statue of Boudicca stands on Westminster Bridge, close to the Houses of Parliament.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Iceni, led by Boudicca, lived in what is now which part of England?',array['The West Midlands','South-west England','Eastern England','Northern England']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Boudicca ruled the Iceni, a tribe in present-day eastern England.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who were the Picts?',array['Ancestors of the Scottish people','Roman soldiers from Gaul','Ancestors of the Welsh people','A tribe from Denmark']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Hadrian''s Wall was built to keep out the Picts, ancestors of the Scots.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What status does Hadrian''s Wall have?',array['National park','Crown dependency','UNESCO World Heritage Site','Royal park']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Hadrian''s Wall is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and popular with walkers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How long did the Romans remain in Britain?',array['100 years','400 years','200 years','1,000 years']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Roman rule in Britain lasted about four centuries.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides roads and public buildings, what did the Romans create in Britain?',array['The first printed books','The Domesday Book','A structure of law','The first Parliament']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Romans built roads and public buildings and set up a structure of law.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Romans introduced new plants and animals to Britain.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Among their contributions, the Romans brought new plants and animals to Britain.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did the first Christian communities appear in Britain?',array['The 3rd and 4th centuries AD','The 7th century AD','The 11th century AD','The 1st century BC']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Christian communities first appeared in Britain during the 3rd and 4th centuries AD, under the Romans.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did the Roman army leave Britain in AD 410?',array['To defend other parts of the Roman Empire','Because of a deadly plague','After defeat by Boudicca','After defeat by the Vikings']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The army left to defend other areas of the Empire and never came back.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The languages of the Jutes, Angles and Saxons form the basis of:',array['Norman French','Scottish Gaelic','modern Welsh','modern English']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The languages of these invaders are the foundation of modern-day English.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By about which year were Anglo-Saxon kingdoms established in Britain?',array['AD 410','AD 789','AD 43','AD 600']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Despite resistance, Anglo-Saxon kingdoms were established by around AD 600.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which parts of Britain remained free of Anglo-Saxon rule?',array['Kent and Sussex','London and the south-east','East Anglia and Suffolk','Much of Wales and Scotland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Anglo-Saxon kingdoms were mainly in England; much of Wales and Scotland stayed free.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Anglo-Saxons were already Christians when they first came to Britain.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Anglo-Saxons were not Christian on arrival; missionaries later converted them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which part of Britain did missionaries from Ireland spread Christianity?',array['The east','The south','The south-west','The north']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Irish missionaries spread Christianity in the north, while those from Rome worked in the south.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which missionary would become the patron saint of Ireland?',array['St Andrew','St Augustine','St Patrick','St Columba']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): St Patrick, one of the missionaries from Ireland, became Ireland''s patron saint.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did the missionaries led by St Augustine come from?',array['Ireland','Normandy','Rome','Iona']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): St Augustine brought missionaries from Rome and they spread Christianity in the south of Britain.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the Vikings take away when they raided coastal towns?',array['Books and maps','Cattle and horses','Land and castles','Goods and slaves']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Vikings first came in AD 789 to raid coastal towns and carry off goods and slaves.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did the Vikings begin to form their own communities?',array['The east of England and Scotland','The south coast of England','The west of Wales','Cornwall and Devon']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After raiding, the Vikings began to settle in the east of England and of Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was the area of Viking settlement in the east and north of England called?',array['The Marches','The Lowlands','The Danelaw','The Pale']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Many Viking invaders stayed in an area of England known as the Danelaw.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Some Viking settlers mixed with local communities and converted to Christianity.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Viking settlers mingled with local people and some became Christians.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who was the first of the Danish kings who briefly ruled England?',array['Harold','Alfred the Great','Cnut (also called Canute)','Kenneth MacAlpin']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): For a short period England had Danish kings, the first being Cnut, or Canute.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What encouraged the people in the north to unite under Kenneth MacAlpin?',array['The Black Death','The threat of Viking attacks','The Roman invasion','The Norman Conquest']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): Fear of Viking attack led northern peoples to unite under one king.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Normandy, from where William invaded England in 1066, is in what is now:',array['Denmark','northern France','Belgium','southern France']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): William was Duke of Normandy, in present-day northern France.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','By what name is William, who became king of England in 1066, known?',array['William the Bold','William the Great','William the Lionheart','William the Conqueror']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): After defeating Harold at Hastings, William became king and is called William the Conqueror.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where can the Bayeux Tapestry be seen today?',array['In London','In France','In Canterbury','In Edinburgh']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Bayeux Tapestry, which commemorates the Battle of Hastings, is still on view in France.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of object is the Bayeux Tapestry?',array['A stone carving','An illustrated book','A large piece of embroidery','A stained glass window']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The battle of 1066 is remembered in a great piece of embroidery, the Bayeux Tapestry.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did the Norman Conquest lead to in England?',array['Union with Scotland','The end of the monarchy','Many changes in government and social structures','The building of Hadrian''s Wall']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Conquest brought major changes to England''s government and society.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: After the Normans first conquered Wales, the Welsh never won back any territory.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Normans initially conquered Wales, but the Welsh gradually regained land.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which king had the Domesday Book drawn up?',array['Alfred the Great','William the Conqueror','King John','Edward I']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): William sent people across England to list towns, villages, landowners and animals in the Domesday Book.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S3.7-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Domesday Book has been lost and no longer exists.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 3 (A long and illustrious history): The Domesday Book still exists and shows English society just after the Norman Conquest.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_history']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Since which year has the BBC organised the Proms?',array['1966','1897','1927','1945']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The BBC has run the Proms, a summer season of orchestral concerts, since 1927.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which concert is the best-known event of the Proms season?',array['The Last Night of the Proms','The Proms Midsummer Gala','The First Night of the Proms','The Proms Christmas Concert']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The final concert, the Last Night of the Proms, is the season''s most famous and is televised.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Henry Purcell is remembered for developing:',array['the Music for the Royal Fireworks','the Pomp and Circumstance Marches','the Aldeburgh music festival','a distinctly British style of music']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Purcell, organist at Westminster Abbey, created a British musical style that differed from the rest of Europe and still influences British composers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did the German-born composer Handel become a British citizen?',array['1688','1801','1759','1727']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): George Frederick Handel lived in the UK for many years and took British citizenship in 1727.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','For which king did Handel compose Music for the Royal Fireworks?',array['George II','Charles II','George III','George I']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Handel composed the Water Music for George I and, for his son George II, Music for the Royal Fireworks.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Handel''s oratorio Messiah is often sung by choirs around which festival?',array['Bonfire Night','Halloween','Easter','Valentine''s Day']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Messiah is regularly performed by choirs, often at Easter time.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which composer wrote The Planets, a suite themed on the planets of the solar system?',array['Gustav Holst','Sir William Walton','Benjamin Britten','Sir Edward Elgar']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Gustav Holst (1874-1934) composed The Planets suite.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The tune of the hymn I vow to thee my country was adapted from which piece?',array['The Water Music','Façade','Jupiter, from The Planets','Land of Hope and Glory']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Holst adapted Jupiter, part of his Planets suite, as the tune for this popular church hymn.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which English city was the composer Sir Edward Elgar born?',array['Sheffield','Worcester','Manchester','Bath']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Elgar, composer of the Pomp and Circumstance Marches, was born in Worcester.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which composer was strongly influenced by traditional English folk music?',array['Henry Purcell','George Frederick Handel','Ralph Vaughan Williams','Sir William Walton']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Ralph Vaughan Williams wrote for orchestras and choirs and drew heavily on English folk music.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which composer wrote coronation marches for both George VI and Elizabeth II?',array['Sir William Walton','Henry Purcell','Gustav Holst','George Frederick Handel']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sir William Walton (1902-83) composed coronation marches for George VI and Elizabeth II.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which composer wrote Façade (later a ballet) and Balthazar''s Feast?',array['Sir William Walton','Sir Edward Elgar','Gustav Holst','Benjamin Britten']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Walton''s best-known works are probably Façade and Balthazar''s Feast, written for a large choir.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO operas were composed by Benjamin Britten?',array['HMS Pinafore and The Mikado','Peter Grimes and Evita','The Mikado and Billy Budd','Peter Grimes and Billy Budd']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britten is best known for operas such as Peter Grimes and Billy Budd; The Mikado and HMS Pinafore are Gilbert and Sullivan, and Evita is Lloyd Webber.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Britten''s A Young Person''s Guide to the Orchestra is based on a piece by which composer?',array['Henry Purcell','Sir Edward Elgar','Gustav Holst','George Frederick Handel']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britten based this introduction to the sections of the orchestra on music by Purcell.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Benjamin Britten founded an internationally important music festival in which Suffolk town?',array['Aldeburgh','Greenwich','Worcester','Glastonbury']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britten founded the Aldeburgh festival in Suffolk, which remains a popular event.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which is one reason the handbook gives for Britain''s impact on popular music around the world?',array['The size of the UK''s population','Government grants for pop bands','The UK''s large number of opera houses','The wide use of the English language']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britain''s influence is linked to the spread of English, its cultural links with many countries and its inventiveness.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO British bands does the handbook name as still influencing music at home and abroad?',array['The Beatles and The Rolling Stones','Gilbert and Sullivan and The Beatles','Monty Python and The Rolling Stones','The Beatles and Morecambe and Wise']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Since the 1960s bands such as The Beatles and The Rolling Stones have influenced music in the UK and abroad.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When did the Punk movement appear in British pop music?',array['The late 1980s','The late 1970s','The early 1950s','The early 2000s']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British pop kept innovating, for example with Punk in the late 1970s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which trend in British pop music is associated with the 1990s?',array['Gilbert and Sullivan revivals','The Punk movement','Music hall variety acts','Boy and girl bands']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The 1990s saw a trend towards boy bands and girl bands.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which part of London is the large music venue The O2?',array['Pimlico','Greenwich','Whitehall','Chelsea']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The O2 is in Greenwich, south-east London, and hosts music events throughout the year.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, the Scottish Exhibition and Conference Centre (SECC), a major music venue, is in which city?',array['Dundee','Aberdeen','Glasgow','Edinburgh']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The SECC in Glasgow is named alongside Wembley Stadium and The O2 as a large music venue.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are famous UK summer music festivals?',array['Glastonbury and the Chelsea Flower Show','The Turner Prize and the Isle of Wight Festival','Glastonbury and the Isle of Wight Festival','Hogmanay and the Chelsea Flower Show']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Famous summer festivals named in the handbook include Glastonbury, the V Festival and the Isle of Wight Festival.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','As well as music, dance and art, the National Eisteddfod of Wales holds important competitions for:',array['Welsh poetry','short films','Welsh cookery','garden design']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Eisteddfod is an annual Welsh cultural festival that includes major Welsh poetry competitions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Mercury Music Prize is awarded for:',array['the best British film soundtrack','the best live festival performance','the best new musical in the West End','the best album from the UK and Ireland']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Each year the Mercury Music Prize recognises the best album from the UK and Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which month is the Mercury Music Prize awarded?',array['December','September','February','May']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Mercury Music Prize is presented each September.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which annual event awards music prizes like best British group and best British solo artist?',array['The Brit Awards','The Turner Prize','The Mercury Music Prize','The Laurence Olivier Awards']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Brit Awards are held every year and cover many categories, for groups and solo artists alike.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','London''s West End is also known as:',array['Stageland','Playville','Theatreland','Showtown']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): London''s West End theatre district is often called ''Theatreland''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which play holds the record for the longest first run of any show?',array['Cats','The Mousetrap','The Phantom of the Opera','The Mikado']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Agatha Christie''s murder mystery The Mousetrap holds the record for the longest initial run.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Since which year has The Mousetrap been running in the West End?',array['1927','1968','1984','1952']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Mousetrap opened in the West End in 1952.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who wrote the murder-mystery play The Mousetrap?',array['Dame Agatha Christie','Harold Pinter','J K Rowling','Sir Arthur Conan Doyle']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Mousetrap is by Dame Agatha Christie, whose detective stories are read worldwide.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Gilbert and Sullivan wrote their comic operas in the 20th century.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Gilbert and Sullivan wrote their comic operas, often mocking politics and popular culture, in the 19th century.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a comic opera by Gilbert and Sullivan?',array['Peter Grimes','Billy Budd','Evita','The Pirates of Penzance']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Gilbert and Sullivan''s operas include HMS Pinafore, The Pirates of Penzance and The Mikado.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Andrew Lloyd Webber worked with which collaborator on Jesus Christ Superstar and Evita?',array['Ian Fleming','Harold Pinter','Arthur Sullivan','Tim Rice']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Lloyd Webber wrote the music for Jesus Christ Superstar and Evita in collaboration with Tim Rice.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At what time of year do many British theatres put on a pantomime?',array['Halloween','Midsummer','Christmas','Easter']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Pantomimes are light-hearted family shows based on fairy stories, usually staged at Christmas.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In a pantomime, the traditional character known as the Dame is:',array['a fairy played by a child','a horse played by two actors','a man played by a woman','a woman played by a man']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Dame is a female character played by a male actor; pantomime animals are played by two actors in one costume.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the biggest and best-known part of the Edinburgh Festival?',array['The Edinburgh Festival Fringe','The Proms','The National Eisteddfod','Hogmanay']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Edinburgh Festival is a series of summer arts festivals, the largest being the Fringe.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Edinburgh Festival Fringe mainly showcases which kinds of performance?',array['Theatre and comedy','Opera and ballet','Film and animation','Classical and choral music']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Fringe is mostly theatre and comedy and often includes experimental work.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which city are the Laurence Olivier Awards held each year?',array['Cardiff','London','Manchester','Edinburgh']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Olivier Awards are held every year in London, at varying venues.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Sir Laurence Olivier, after whom theatre awards are named, was best known for roles in plays by:',array['William Shakespeare','Harold Pinter','Gilbert and Sullivan','Agatha Christie']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The awards honour the actor Sir Laurence (later Lord) Olivier, famous for his Shakespearean roles.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','During the Middle Ages, most art in Britain had what kind of theme?',array['Religious','Political','Scientific','Military']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Medieval art was mostly religious, such as church wall paintings and illustrated religious books.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What happened to much medieval religious art after the Protestant Reformation?',array['It went to Tate Britain','Much of it was lost','It was moved to Rome','It was sold to America']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Much religious art was lost after the Reformation, while wealthy families began collecting other paintings and sculptures.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO foreign-born painters worked in Britain in the 1500s and 1600s?',array['Hans Holbein and Thomas Gainsborough','John Constable and Sir Anthony Van Dyck','Hans Holbein and Sir Anthony Van Dyck','Joseph Turner and David Allan']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Many painters in 16th- and 17th-century Britain were foreign, for example Holbein and Van Dyck; British painters became well known from the 18th century.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which city is the National Gallery of Scotland?',array['Aberdeen','Glasgow','Edinburgh','Inverness']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The National Gallery of Scotland, in Edinburgh, is one of the UK''s best-known galleries.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The National Museum named in the handbook as a well-known gallery is in which city?',array['Bangor','Wrexham','Swansea','Cardiff']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The National Museum in Cardiff is listed among the UK''s best-known galleries.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO well-known art galleries are in London?',array['Tate Modern and the National Museum','Tate Britain and Tate Modern','The National Museum and the National Gallery of Scotland','The National Gallery of Scotland and Tate Britain']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): London''s galleries include the National Gallery, Tate Britain and Tate Modern; the National Museum is in Cardiff and Scotland''s National Gallery is in Edinburgh.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was the Turner Prize established?',array['1984','1952','1968','1927']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Turner Prize, named after Joseph Turner, was set up in 1984.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of art does the Turner Prize celebrate?',array['Classical portrait painting','Contemporary art','Medieval religious art','Landscape gardening']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Turner Prize recognises contemporary art and is one of Europe''s most prestigious visual art awards.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many works are shortlisted for the Turner Prize each year?',array['Six','Three','Ten','Four']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Four works make the shortlist every year.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where are the Turner Prize shortlisted works shown before the winner is announced?',array['Tate Britain','The Royal Albert Hall','Tate Modern','The National Gallery']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The four shortlisted works are exhibited at Tate Britain before the winner is named.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which artist is named in the handbook as a past winner of the Turner Prize?',array['Lucian Freud','David Hockney','Damien Hirst','Henry Moore']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Damien Hirst and Richard Wright are given as previous Turner Prize winners.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The portrait painter Thomas Gainsborough often painted people against a background of:',array['battlefields','stormy seascapes','busy city streets','country or garden scenery']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Thomas Gainsborough (1727-88) often set his portrait subjects in country or garden scenery.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','David Allan, a painter best known for portraits, came from which country?',array['Scotland','Germany','Wales','Northern Ireland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): David Allan (1744-96) was a Scottish portrait painter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Origin of Painting is one of the most famous works of which artist?',array['John Constable','David Allan','Sir John Lavery','Thomas Gainsborough']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): One of the Scottish painter David Allan''s best-known works is The Origin of Painting.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which artist is considered to have raised the profile of landscape painting?',array['Sir John Lavery','David Allan','Lucian Freud','Joseph Turner']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Turner painted landscapes in a modern style and is credited with raising their status.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which artist was a member of the Pre-Raphaelites?',array['Dante Gabriel Rossetti','David Hockney','Thomas Gainsborough','Hans Holbein']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Pre-Raphaelites included Holman Hunt, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and Sir John Millais.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The Pre-Raphaelites painted detailed pictures in bright colours on religious or literary themes.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): This group of artists, active in the second half of the 19th century, painted detailed, brightly coloured religious and literary scenes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The successful portrait painter Sir John Lavery came from:',array['Scotland','Germany','Northern Ireland','Wales']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sir John Lavery (1856-1941) was a Northern Irish portrait painter whose subjects included the Royal Family.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Welsh artist John Petts is best known for:',array['Art Deco ceramics','pop art paintings','engravings and stained glass','large bronze abstract sculptures']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): John Petts (1914-91) was a Welsh artist known for engravings and stained glass.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Lucian Freud, a British artist best known for his portraits, was born in which country?',array['Germany','Wales','Scotland','France']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Freud, famous for portraits, was born in Germany and became a British artist.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','David Hockney was an important contributor to which 1960s art movement?',array['Pop art','Art Deco','The Gothic revival','The Pre-Raphaelites']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): David Hockney played an important part in the 1960s pop art movement and remains influential.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is given in the handbook as an example of a great medieval cathedral?',array['The Banqueting House','St Pancras Station','Dumfries House','Durham Cathedral']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Medieval cathedrals still standing include those in Durham, Lincoln, Canterbury and Salisbury.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of building is the White Tower at the Tower of London?',array['A Norman castle keep','A Roman lighthouse','A Tudor royal palace','A Gothic cathedral']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The White Tower is a Norman keep built on William the Conqueror''s orders.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Hardwick Hall, an example of a great country house, is in which county?',array['Essex','Derbyshire','Suffolk','Worcestershire']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hardwick Hall in Derbyshire is one of the great country houses built as landowners became wealthier.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-064','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which 17th-century architect designed the Queen''s House at Greenwich?',array['Sir Christopher Wren','Sir Edwin Lutyens','Inigo Jones','Robert Adam']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Inigo Jones drew on classical architecture; his buildings include the Queen''s House (Greenwich) and the Banqueting House (Whitehall).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Inigo Jones took inspiration from which kind of architecture?',array['Art Deco','Gothic','Norman','Classical']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Jones drew on classical architecture for buildings such as the Banqueting House.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The 18th-century architect Robert Adam came from:',array['Wales','Northern Ireland','Scotland','England']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Scottish architect Robert Adam influenced architecture in the UK, Europe and America.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Dumfries House in Scotland is an example of the work of which architect?',array['Sir Norman Foster','Inigo Jones','Sir Edwin Lutyens','Robert Adam']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Robert Adam designed great houses such as Dumfries House, including their interior decoration.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Robert Adam designed only the outside of his buildings and left the interior decoration to others.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Adam designed the inside decoration of great houses as well as the buildings themselves.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-069','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Royal Crescent, built under the influence of Robert Adam''s ideas, is in which city?',array['Bath','Durham','Edinburgh','York']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Robert Adam''s ideas shaped architecture in Bath, home of the Royal Crescent.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-070','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO buildings were built in the 19th-century gothic style?',array['The Banqueting House and the Houses of Parliament','The Queen''s House and St Pancras Station','The White Tower and Hardwick Hall','The Houses of Parliament and St Pancras Station']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): In the 19th century the medieval gothic style returned, used for the Houses of Parliament, St Pancras Station and many town halls.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-071','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Town halls in which city are given as examples of the 19th-century gothic revival?',array['Sheffield','Bath','Worcester','Greenwich']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): As cities grew in the 19th century, gothic-style public buildings were put up, including town halls in Manchester and Sheffield.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-072','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Sir Edwin Lutyens designed which city as the seat of government in India?',array['Mumbai','Madras','New Delhi','Calcutta']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Lutyens, influential across the Empire, designed New Delhi as India''s seat of government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-073','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which war memorial in Whitehall did Sir Edwin Lutyens design?',array['The Royal Crescent','The Banqueting House','The Cenotaph','The White Tower']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): After the First World War Lutyens designed many war memorials, including the Cenotaph.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-074','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a modern British architect working on major projects around the world?',array['Robert Adam','Sir Norman Foster','Inigo Jones','Lancelot ''Capability'' Brown']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Modern British architects named in the handbook include Sir Norman Foster, Lord (Richard) Rogers and Dame Zaha Hadid.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-075','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Why did Lancelot Brown get the nickname ''Capability''?',array['He could design any kind of building','He was a capable army engineer','He trained many other gardeners','He often said a place had ''capabilities''']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Brown, the 18th-century landscape designer, often told clients a place had ''capabilities''.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-076','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which garden designer often worked with Sir Edwin Lutyens?',array['Gertrude Jekyll','Vivienne Westwood','Mary Quant','Clarice Cliff']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Gertrude Jekyll created colourful gardens around houses designed by Lutyens.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-077','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What did Thomas Chippendale design in the 18th century?',array['Ceramics','Gardens','Clothing','Furniture']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Thomas Chippendale was an 18th-century furniture designer.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-078','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Clarice Cliff is known for designing:',array['landscape gardens','Art Deco ceramics','18th-century furniture','stained glass windows']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Clarice Cliff designed Art Deco ceramics.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-079','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Sir Terence Conran was a 20th-century:',array['interior designer','fashion photographer','film director','landscape painter']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sir Terence Conran is named as a 20th-century interior designer.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-080','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is named as a leading British fashion designer of recent years?',array['Clarice Cliff','Thomas Chippendale','Alexander McQueen','Sir Terence Conran']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Leading recent fashion designers include Mary Quant, Alexander McQueen and Vivienne Westwood.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-081','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which British playwright has won the Nobel Prize in Literature?',array['Andrew Lloyd Webber','Harold Pinter','Tim Rice','Sir William Walton']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British Nobel literature winners include novelist William Golding, poet Seamus Heaney and playwright Harold Pinter.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-082','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Seamus Heaney, a winner of the Nobel Prize in Literature, is described in the handbook as a:',array['playwright','novelist','poet','composer']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Heaney is listed as the poet among British Nobel literature laureates.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-083','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which novelist is named among British winners of the Nobel Prize in Literature?',array['J K Rowling','Evelyn Waugh','Sir William Golding','Ian Fleming']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The novelist Sir William Golding is one of the British writers who have won the Nobel Prize in Literature.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-084','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Ian Fleming''s books introduced which fictional character?',array['James Bond','Mr Micawber','Sherlock Holmes','Harry Potter']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Ian Fleming created James Bond.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-085','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Since which year has the Man Booker Prize for Fiction been awarded?',array['1984','1927','1952','1968']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Man Booker Prize has been awarded every year since 1968.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-086','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, the Man Booker Prize is open to authors from:',array['the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe','England and Wales only','the USA and Canada only','the UK only']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The handbook limits eligibility to writers from the Commonwealth, Ireland or Zimbabwe.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-087','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which author is named as a past winner of the Man Booker Prize?',array['Hilary Mantel','Charles Dickens','Jane Austen','Thomas Hardy']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Past winners include Ian McEwan, Hilary Mantel and Julian Barnes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-088','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Jane Austen''s novels are mainly concerned with:',array['detectives solving crimes','rural poverty and religion','life in the trenches of war','marriage and family relationships']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Austen''s novels focus on marriage and family life, and many have been adapted for film and television.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-089','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','If someone is called a ''Scrooge'', after a Charles Dickens character, it means they are:',array['very brave','mean','very clever','always hopeful']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Dickens''s characters appear in everyday speech: a Scrooge is a mean person, while Mr Micawber stands for someone always hopeful.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-090','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which novel was written by Charles Dickens?',array['Kidnapped','Lucky Jim','Great Expectations','Brideshead Revisited']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Dickens''s famous novels include Oliver Twist and Great Expectations.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-091','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO books were written by Robert Louis Stevenson?',array['Brighton Rock and Kidnapped','Treasure Island and Scoop','Treasure Island and Kidnapped','Lucky Jim and Jude the Obscure']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Stevenson''s best-known books are Treasure Island, Kidnapped and Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-092','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Thomas Hardy''s best-known novels mainly focus on:',array['rural society','the First World War','detectives and crime','life in London''s slums']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hardy, a novelist and poet, wrote mainly about rural society.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-093','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which novel was written by Thomas Hardy?',array['Far from the Madding Crowd','Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde','Our Man in Havana','Decline and Fall']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hardy''s novels include Far from the Madding Crowd and Jude the Obscure.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-094','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Sherlock Holmes was one of the first detectives in fiction.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sir Arthur Conan Doyle''s Sherlock Holmes was among the earliest fictional detectives.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-095','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Evelyn Waugh is perhaps best known for which novel?',array['Lucky Jim','Brighton Rock','Jude the Obscure','Brideshead Revisited']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Waugh wrote satirical novels such as Decline and Fall and Scoop, but is best known for Brideshead Revisited.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-096','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which is the best-known novel by Sir Kingsley Amis?',array['Scoop','The Honorary Consul','Lucky Jim','Kidnapped']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Kingsley Amis wrote over 20 novels, the most famous being Lucky Jim.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-097','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Graham Greene''s novels were often influenced by his:',array['army service','childhood in India','work as a doctor','religious beliefs']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Greene''s novels, such as Brighton Rock and The Heart of the Matter, often reflect his religious beliefs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-098','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which novel was written by Graham Greene?',array['Lucky Jim','Scoop','Our Man in Havana','Treasure Island']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Greene''s novels include The Heart of the Matter, The Honorary Consul, Brighton Rock and Our Man in Havana.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-099','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who wrote the Harry Potter series of children''s books?',array['JRR Tolkien','Jane Austen','J K Rowling','Agatha Christie']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): J K Rowling''s Harry Potter books have been hugely successful worldwide; she now also writes for adults.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-100','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the Anglo-Saxon poem Beowulf, the hero fights against:',array['the Normans','the Vikings','the Romans','monsters']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Beowulf describes a hero fighting monsters and is still translated into modern English.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-101','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The medieval poem Sir Gawain and the Green Knight is about a knight at the court of:',array['King Alfred','Henry VIII','William the Conqueror','King Arthur']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): This poem from the Middle Ages concerns one of King Arthur''s knights.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-102','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many lines must a sonnet have?',array['12','16','10','14']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Shakespeare wrote many sonnets, poems that must have 14 lines.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-103','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which poet, inspired by his Protestant religious views, wrote Paradise Lost?',array['William Blake','Lord Byron','John Milton','John Keats']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): As Protestant ideas spread, poets such as John Milton wrote religious poetry, including Paradise Lost.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-104','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides Scotland itself, Sir Walter Scott''s poems drew on traditional stories and songs from:',array['the Isle of Wight','the valleys of south Wales','the Lake District','the border area of Scotland and England']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Scott drew on Scotland and the stories and songs of the Scottish-English borders; he also wrote novels set in Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-105','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Sir Walter Scott wrote poetry but never wrote any novels.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Besides his poems, Scott wrote novels, many of them set in Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-106','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO were popular poets of the 19th century?',array['John Keats and Gustav Holst','John Keats and Percy Shelley','Thomas Chippendale and John Constable','Joseph Turner and Percy Shelley']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): 19th-century poets include Blake, Keats, Byron, Shelley, Tennyson and the Brownings; Holst was a composer and Turner and Constable were painters.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-107','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Like Wilfred Owen, which poet wrote about his experiences in the First World War?',array['John Milton','Siegfried Sassoon','Sir John Betjeman','Lord Byron']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Wilfred Owen and Siegfried Sassoon both wrote poetry about the First World War.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-108','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is named as a more recent popular British poet?',array['Geoffrey Chaucer','Ted Hughes','William Blake','John Milton']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): More recent popular poets include Walter de la Mare, John Masefield, Sir John Betjeman and Ted Hughes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-109','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where are some of the best-known British poets buried or commemorated?',array['The crypt of St Paul''s Cathedral','Tate Britain','Canterbury Cathedral','Poets'' Corner in Westminster Abbey']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Many famous poets are buried or remembered in Poets'' Corner, Westminster Abbey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-110','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which poem by Robert Browning begins ''Oh to be in England now that April''s there''?',array['Home Thoughts from Abroad','The Tyger','Anthem for Doomed Youth','She Walks in Beauty']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): These lines open Robert Browning''s Home Thoughts from Abroad.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-111','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who wrote the poem She Walks in Beauty?',array['Robert Browning','Wilfred Owen','William Blake','Lord Byron']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): She Walks in Beauty is by Lord Byron (1788-1824).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-112','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which poet wrote The Tyger, which begins ''Tyger! Tyger! Burning bright''?',array['William Blake','William Wordsworth','Robert Browning','Lord Byron']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Tyger is by William Blake (1757-1827).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.2-113','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The line ''What passing-bells for these who die as cattle?'' comes from which poem?',array['Anthem for Doomed Youth','The Daffodils','Home Thoughts from Abroad','She Walks in Beauty']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The line opens Wilfred Owen''s First World War poem Anthem for Doomed Youth.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Many Christians go to church on Christmas Eve, which falls on:',array['23 December','31 December','24 December','26 December']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Christmas Eve is 24 December; many Christians attend church then or on Christmas Day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is traditionally part of a special Christmas meal?',array['Ulster fry','Haggis','Roast turkey','Pancakes']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): A traditional Christmas dinner may feature roast turkey, Christmas pudding and mince pies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Father Christmas, who young children believe brings presents, is also called:',array['Santa Claus','The Dame','Old Father Time','Jack Frost']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Very young children believe Father Christmas, or Santa Claus, brings presents on the night before Christmas Day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which months does Easter take place?',array['May or June','January or February','October or November','March or April']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Easter falls in March or April.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO days around Easter are public holidays?',array['Ash Wednesday and Shrove Tuesday','Shrove Tuesday and Good Friday','Good Friday and Easter Monday','Ash Wednesday and Easter Monday']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Both Good Friday and the Monday after Easter Sunday (Easter Monday) are public holidays.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What do many people do during Lent today?',array['Exchange cards and gifts with lovers','Set off fireworks in their gardens','Give something up, such as a favourite food','Play jokes on each other until midday']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): People traditionally fasted in Lent; today many give up something, like a favourite food.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Pancakes were traditionally made on Shrove Tuesday in order to:',array['use up eggs, milk and fat before the fast','celebrate the end of the harvest','mark the start of the new year','remember the Gunpowder Plot']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Making pancakes used up rich foods like eggs, fat and milk before the Lent fast.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which day does Lent begin?',array['Ash Wednesday','Good Friday','Shrove Tuesday','Easter Monday']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Lent starts on Ash Wednesday, the day after Shrove Tuesday.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At Ash Wednesday services, the ash cross marked on Christians'' foreheads is a symbol of:',array['death and sorrow for sin','the victory of good over evil','new life and hope','the birth of Jesus']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The ash cross stands for death and sorrow for sin.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Chocolate eggs given at Easter, including by non-religious people, are a symbol of:',array['friendship','new life','the harvest','good luck']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Easter eggs are given as presents to symbolise new life.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many days does Diwali last?',array['Two','Forty','Eight','Five']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Diwali, usually in October or November, lasts five days.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Diwali is often called:',array['the Festival of Harvest','the Festival of Freedom','the Festival of Spring','the Festival of Lights']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Diwali is widely known as the Festival of Lights.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Diwali celebrates the victory of good over evil and:',array['the founding of the Khalsa','the end of Ramadan','the gaining of knowledge','the birth of Jesus Christ']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Diwali marks good triumphing over evil and the gaining of knowledge.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which months does Hannukah take place?',array['November or December','June or July','August or September','March or April']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hannukah is held in November or December and lasts eight days.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','During Hannukah, a candle is lit each day on a stand of eight candles called a:',array['chalice','Khalsa','menorah','pagoda']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The eight-candle stand used at Hannukah is called a menorah.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Hannukah story tells of oil that should have lasted one day but instead lasted:',array['a whole month','eight days','forty days','five days']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Candles are lit to recall oil meant for a single day that lasted for eight.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Eid al-Fitr takes place on the same date every year.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The date of Eid al-Fitr changes from year to year.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At Eid al-Fitr, Muslims thank Allah for:',array['the strength to complete the fast','a good harvest','victory in battle','the founding of the Khalsa']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): After a month of fasting in Ramadan, Muslims give thanks for the strength to finish it.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In Britain, an animal sacrificed during Eid ul Adha must be killed:',array['in a slaughterhouse','at home','by a vet','at a mosque']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Many Muslims sacrifice an animal to eat at Eid ul Adha; in Britain this must happen in a slaughterhouse.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which date is the Sikh festival Vaisakhi celebrated each year?',array['5 November','1 March','14 February','14 April']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Vaisakhi (Baisakhi) is celebrated every year on 14 April.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How is Vaisakhi celebrated?',array['With fireworks and bonfires','With pancakes and eggs','With parades, dancing and singing','With jokes until midday']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Vaisakhi celebrations include parades, dancing and singing.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Apart from 1 January, which day is also a public holiday in Scotland at New Year?',array['6 January','31 December','3 January','2 January']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): In Scotland 2 January is a public holiday as well as New Year''s Day.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Some people in Scotland consider Hogmanay a bigger holiday than Christmas.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hogmanay (31 December) matters more than Christmas to some people in Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which date is Valentine''s Day?',array['1 March','14 February','1 February','14 April']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): On Valentine''s Day, 14 February, lovers exchange cards and gifts.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On Valentine''s Day, people sometimes send anonymous cards to:',array['their teachers','their neighbours','their grandparents','someone they secretly admire']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Some people send unsigned Valentine cards to a secret admirer''s object of affection.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Television programmes and newspapers sometimes include joke stories on April Fool''s Day.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): On 1 April the media often run stories that are April Fool jokes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When is Father''s Day?',array['The last Sunday in July','The third Sunday in June','The Sunday three weeks before Easter','The first Sunday in May']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Father''s Day falls on the third Sunday in June, when children send cards or gifts to their fathers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At Halloween, young people often dress up in frightening costumes to play:',array['pancake races','''trick or treat''','musical chairs','hide and seek']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): On 31 October young people in scary costumes play ''trick or treat'', and people give treats to avoid tricks.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At Halloween, many people make lanterns with a candle inside by carving:',array['apples','potatoes','pumpkins','melons']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Carving pumpkins into lanterns is a common Halloween custom.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who led the group of Catholics whose failed plot is remembered on Bonfire Night?',array['Robert the Bruce','Guy Fawkes','Oliver Cromwell','Charles Edward Stuart']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Bonfire Night recalls the failed 1605 plot by Catholics led by Guy Fawkes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How do people in Great Britain usually mark Bonfire Night?',array['By wearing poppies','By lighting candles on a menorah','By eating pancakes','By setting off fireworks']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): On 5 November people set off fireworks at home or attend public displays.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Remembrance Day originally commemorated those who died in the Second World War.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Remembrance Day first commemorated the dead of the First World War; it now honours all those killed while fighting for the UK and its allies.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How long is the silence held at 11.00 am on Remembrance Day?',array['One minute','Two minutes','Ten minutes','Five minutes']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): A two-minute silence is held at 11.00 am and wreaths are laid at the Cenotaph.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Apart from May, in which month is there a bank holiday throughout the UK?',array['September','August','October','March']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): There are bank holidays in early May, at the end of May or start of June, and in August.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In Northern Ireland, an extra public holiday in July marks the anniversary of:',array['the Battle of Bannockburn','the Battle of Hastings','the Battle of the Boyne','the Battle of Britain']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Northern Ireland has a public holiday in July for the anniversary of the Battle of the Boyne.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.3-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On a bank holiday, which of these are usually closed for the day?',array['Only churches','Only schools','Banks and many other businesses','Only government offices']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Bank holidays are public holidays when banks and many other businesses close.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is an ''allotment''?',array['Extra land rented for growing fruit and vegetables','A small local flower show','A public park in a town centre','A shop selling plants and garden tools']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Some people rent an additional plot of land, called an allotment, to grow fruit and vegetables.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which part of the UK is Bodnant Garden?',array['Wales','Scotland','England','Northern Ireland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Bodnant Garden in Wales is one of the UK''s famous gardens.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Mount Stewart is a famous garden in:',array['Northern Ireland','Scotland','England','Wales']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Mount Stewart is the Northern Irish garden named in the handbook.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO famous gardens are in Scotland?',array['Bodnant Garden and Mount Stewart','Crathes Castle and Hidcote','Sissinghurst and Inveraray Castle','Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Crathes Castle and Inveraray Castle are in Scotland; Sissinghurst and Hidcote are in England, Bodnant in Wales and Mount Stewart in Northern Ireland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these famous gardens is in England?',array['Mount Stewart','Bodnant Garden','Crathes Castle','Sissinghurst']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): England''s famous gardens include Kew Gardens, Sissinghurst and Hidcote.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the central shopping area of most UK towns and cities called?',array['The city mall','The town centre','The trading quarter','The high market']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): In most towns and cities, the main shopping district is called the town centre.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Most UK shops trade every day of the week, though Sunday hours are usually shorter.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Most shops open every day of the week, with generally reduced hours on Sundays and public holidays.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, why is a wide variety of food eaten in the UK?',array['Its rich cultural heritage and diverse population','Government subsidies for foreign food','Cookery lessons in every school','Laws that require restaurants to offer variety']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The UK''s varied diet reflects its rich cultural heritage and diverse population.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Yorkshire pudding, often served with roast beef, is made of:',array['flour, dried fruit and spices','batter baked in the oven','a sheep''s stomach and oatmeal','potato and soda bread']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Yorkshire puddings are oven-baked batter served with England''s traditional roast beef.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','A traditional snack made from flour, dried fruits and spices, served hot or cold, is associated with:',array['Scotland','Wales','Northern Ireland','England']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Welsh cakes, made from flour, dried fruit and spices, are a traditional Welsh snack.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is part of a traditional Ulster fry?',array['Haggis','Yorkshire pudding','Soda bread','Welsh cakes']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Northern Ireland''s Ulster fry includes bacon, eggs, sausage, black and white pudding, soda bread and potato bread.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is haggis made of?',array['Flour, dried fruit and spices cooked on a griddle','Beef and batter baked together in the oven','Offal, suet, onions and oatmeal in a sheep''s stomach','Bacon, eggs, sausage and fried potato bread']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Haggis, from Scotland, is a sheep''s stomach filled with offal, suet, onions and oatmeal.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Besides roast beef, which dish does the handbook name as popular in England?',array['Fish and chips','Ulster fry','Welsh cakes','Haggis']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Roast beef is England''s traditional dish, and fish and chips are also popular.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year were films first shown publicly in the UK?',array['1927','1896','1876','1935']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Public film screenings began in the UK in 1896 and quickly became popular.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which area of film-making has been a British strength since the early days of cinema?',array['Special effects','Musicals','Westerns','Documentaries']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British film makers were known for clever special effects from the start, and this remains a British speciality.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Charlie Chaplin became famous in silent films for playing which character?',array['A pirate','A detective','A tramp','A spy']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sir Charles (Charlie) Chaplin was famous for his tramp character and made his career in Hollywood.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which decade did British film studios flourish?',array['The 1910s','The 1930s','The 1970s','The 1890s']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British studios flourished in the 1930s, with directors such as Korda and Hitchcock.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which British director later moved to Hollywood and remained an important director there until his death in 1980?',array['Sir David Lean','Sir Alexander Korda','Ridley Scott','Sir Alfred Hitchcock']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Alfred Hitchcock left for Hollywood and worked there until he died in 1980.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','During the Second World War, British films such as In Which We Serve helped to:',array['boost morale','recruit spies','train new soldiers','raise money for taxes']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Wartime British films played an important part in keeping up morale.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which kind of British film enjoyed a high point in the 1950s and 1960s?',array['Westerns','Musicals','Horror films','Comedies']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British comedies such as Passport to Pimlico, The Ladykillers and the Carry On films peaked in the 1950s and 1960s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a British comedy film from the 1950s-1960s high point?',array['The Ladykillers','Lawrence of Arabia','Brief Encounter','The Third Man']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Comedies of that era include Passport to Pimlico, The Ladykillers and the Carry On films.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Many films made in the UK today are produced by foreign companies drawing on British expertise.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Much current UK film production is by foreign companies drawing on British skills.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO highest-grossing film franchises of all time have been produced in the UK?',array['James Bond and Carry On','Harry Potter and Wallace and Gromit','Carry On and Wallace and Gromit','Harry Potter and James Bond']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Harry Potter and James Bond, the two biggest-earning film franchises ever, were both produced in the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which studio can claim to be the world''s oldest film studio facility still in continuous use?',array['Pinewood Studios','Ealing Studios','Shepperton Studios','Elstree Studios']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Ealing Studios has a claim to being the world''s longest continuously operating film studio facility.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, how many Oscars has the animator Nick Park won?',array['Three','Four','Six','Two']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Nick Park has won four Oscars for animated films, three of them for Wallace and Gromit films.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Nick Park won three of his Oscars for animated films featuring:',array['Harry Potter','Monty Python','James Bond','Wallace and Gromit']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Three of Nick Park''s four Oscars were for Wallace and Gromit films, an example of British strength in animation.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these people is named as a recent British Oscar-winning actor?',array['Mike Newell','Ken Russell','Colin Firth','Sir Alexander Korda']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Colin Firth is one of several recent British Oscar winners named, along with Judi Dench and Kate Winslet.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which film was directed by Alfred Hitchcock?',array['The Third Man','Brief Encounter','Chariots of Fire','The 39 Steps']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The 39 Steps (1935) was directed by Alfred Hitchcock.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-029','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO films were directed by David Lean?',array['Brief Encounter and Lawrence of Arabia','Chariots of Fire and Women in Love','Brief Encounter and The Third Man','The 39 Steps and Lawrence of Arabia']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): David Lean directed Brief Encounter (1945) and Lawrence of Arabia (1962).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-030','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who directed The Third Man (1949)?',array['Carol Reed','Ken Russell','David Lean','Nicolas Roeg']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Third Man was directed by Carol Reed.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-031','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Chariots of Fire (1981) was directed by:',array['Hugh Hudson','Roland Joffé','Kevin MacDonald','Mike Newell']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hugh Hudson directed Chariots of Fire.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-032','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which film, directed by Mike Newell, was released in 1994?',array['Touching the Void','Don''t Look Now','Four Weddings and a Funeral','The Killing Fields']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Mike Newell directed Four Weddings and a Funeral (1994).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-033','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who directed The Killing Fields (1984)?',array['Hugh Hudson','Roland Joffé','Frank Launder','Carol Reed']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Killing Fields was directed by Roland Joffé.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-034','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who told jokes and made fun of people at the courts of medieval kings and rich nobles?',array['Pages','Heralds','Bards','Jesters']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Medieval kings and nobles kept jesters to tell jokes and mock people at court.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-035','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which century did political cartoons attacking leading politicians become increasingly popular?',array['The 15th century','The 18th century','The 20th century','The 16th century']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Political cartoons mocking politicians, and sometimes royalty, grew popular in the 18th century.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-036','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which was the most famous satirical magazine first published in the 19th century?',array['Country Life','The Beano','Private Eye','Punch']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Satirical magazines began in the 19th century; the most famous, Punch, first appeared in the 1840s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-037','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which magazine is named as continuing the tradition of satire today?',array['Vogue','The Beano','Private Eye','Country Life']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Political cartoons still appear in newspapers, and Private Eye carries on the satirical tradition.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-038','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What was British music hall?',array['A television talent show','A type of classical concert','A form of variety theatre','A religious music festival']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Music hall was a popular variety theatre featuring comedians, common until television took over.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-039','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which comedy double act performed in music halls before becoming television stars?',array['Wallace and Gromit','Rice and Lloyd Webber','Morecambe and Wise','Gilbert and Sullivan']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Morecambe and Wise performed in music halls in the 1940s and 1950s and went on to television fame.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-040','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which satirical television show was popular in the 1980s and 1990s?',array['Spitting Image','That Was The Week That Was','Coronation Street','EastEnders']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Television satire included Spitting Image (1980s and 1990s) and, earlier, That Was The Week That Was (1960s).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-041','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year did Monty Python''s Flying Circus introduce a new type of progressive comedy?',array['1969','1984','1952','1996']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Monty Python''s Flying Circus began in 1969.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-042','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is stand-up comedy?',array['A comic opera with songs and dances','A satirical show using puppets','A comedy series about a family at home','A solo comedian talking to a live audience']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Stand-up, where a single comedian performs to a live audience, has become popular again.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-043','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which of these is a popular British soap opera?',array['That Was The Week That Was','Spitting Image','Monty Python''s Flying Circus','EastEnders']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Coronation Street and EastEnders are popular regular soaps.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-044','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: There is a television channel in Wales that broadcasts in Welsh.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Wales has a Welsh-language TV channel; Scotland has a Gaelic channel.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-045','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who in the UK must have a television licence?',array['Only households with children','Anyone with a device used to watch TV, such as a TV or computer','Only people who watch BBC channels','Only people who own a television set']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): A licence is needed by everyone with a TV, computer or other equipment used for watching TV.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-046','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In a shared house where people rent rooms on separate tenancy agreements, which is true of TV licences?',array['One licence covers the whole house','No licence is needed for rented rooms','Each tenant must buy a separate licence','Only the landlord needs a licence']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): One licence usually covers a home, but tenants with separate tenancy agreements each need their own.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-047','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, people over what age can apply for a free TV licence?',array['60','75','70','65']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The handbook says people aged over 75 can apply for a free licence.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-048','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What discount on the TV licence can blind people get?',array['25%','50%','100%','75%']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Blind people can get a 50% reduction on the licence fee.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-049','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the maximum fine for watching TV without a licence?',array['£1,000','£5,000','£100','£500']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Watching TV without a licence can lead to a fine of up to £1,000.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-050','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TV licence fees are used to fund:',array['local commercial radio','the National Lottery','all UK television channels','the BBC']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Licence fee income funds the BBC, a public service broadcaster of TV and radio.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-051','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The BBC is funded entirely by the state but is independent of government.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The handbook describes the BBC as the only fully state-funded media organisation independent of government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-052','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How are most UK television channels other than the BBC funded?',array['Through the National Lottery','Through local council tax','Through TV licence fees','Through advertisements and subscriptions']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Apart from the BBC, UK channels are mainly paid for by advertising and subscriptions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-053','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: BBC radio stations are paid for by advertisements.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): BBC radio, like BBC television, is funded by TV licences; other radio stations rely on advertising.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-054','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Some UK radio stations broadcast in regional languages such as:',array['Polish or Urdu','Welsh or Gaelic','French or Spanish','Latin or Greek']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Some radio stations broadcast in Welsh or Gaelic.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-055','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, people use social networking sites like Facebook and Twitter for:',array['voting in general elections','keeping in touch with friends and sharing photos','registering with a doctor','paying for a TV licence']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): People use social networking sites to stay in touch, organise events and share photos, videos and opinions.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-056','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the UK, what is meant by a person''s ''local''?',array['A nearby pub that is a focal point for social life','A neighbourhood corner shop','A community radio station','A local council office']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Most communities have a ''local'' pub that acts as a natural centre for social activities.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-057','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are traditional pub games?',array['Golf and cricket','Pool and darts','Darts and golf','Pool and cricket']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Pool and darts are traditional pub games, and pub quizzes are also popular.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-058','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the minimum age for buying alcohol in a pub or night club?',array['18','21','17','16']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Only people aged 18 or over may buy alcohol in pubs and night clubs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-059','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','At what age may young people have beer or wine with a restaurant meal, if accompanied by someone over 18?',array['17','15','14','16']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): From 16, someone accompanied by an adult over 18 may have beer or wine with a meal in a hotel, restaurant or pub eating area.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-060','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','From what time are pubs usually open on Sundays?',array['11.00 am','10.00 am','2.00 pm','12 noon']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Pubs usually open from 11.00 am, or 12 noon on Sundays.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-061','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who decides the opening hours of a pub or night club?',array['The licensee','The brewery','The government','The local police']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The licensee decides when a pub or night club is open.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-062','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the minimum age for going into a betting shop or gambling club?',array['21','16','17','18']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): You must be 18 to enter betting shops or gambling clubs.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-063','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How often are National Lottery draws made, according to the handbook?',array['Once a month','Every week','Every three months','Once a year']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): National Lottery draws take place every week; you can enter with a ticket or a scratch card.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-065','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: It is legal to neglect a pet as long as you do not treat it cruelly.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The law forbids both cruelty to pets and neglecting them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-066','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In public places, every dog must wear a collar showing:',array['the vet''s phone number','the owner''s name and address','the dog''s date of birth','the dog''s vaccination record']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Dogs in public must wear a collar with the owner''s name and address.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-067','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who is responsible for cleaning up after a dog in a public place?',array['The dog''s owner','The local council','A vet','The police']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Owners must keep their dog under control and clean up after it in public places.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.4-068','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Some charities may help pet owners who cannot afford vet fees.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Vets provide animal vaccinations and treatment, and some charities assist owners unable to pay vet bills.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How many national parks are there in England, Wales and Scotland?',array['20','10','15','12']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): There are 15 national parks across England, Wales and Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Nobody is allowed to live or work inside a UK national park.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): National parks are protected areas that anyone may visit, and people also live and work in them, caring for the landscape.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The UK countryside has a large network of public footpaths.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britain has an extensive system of public footpaths in the countryside, alongside chances for hill walking, mountaineering and mountain biking.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How does the handbook describe the range of museums in the UK?',array['Only large national collections, all based in London','Mainly private collections that are closed to the public','Only small local museums run by volunteers','Anything from small local community museums to big national and civic collections']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): British museums vary in size, from little community museums up to major national and city-run collections.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Most famous landmarks in the UK are:',array['open only to UK citizens','open to the public, generally for a charge','free to visit on every day of the year','closed to the public all year round']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Landmarks are found in towns, cities and the countryside, and most can be visited by the public, usually for a fee.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which year was the National Trust founded?',array['1875','1905','1895','1925']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The National Trust was set up in 1895.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who founded the National Trust?',array['An Act of Parliament','The Church of England','Three volunteers','Queen Victoria']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The National Trust began in 1895 thanks to three volunteers.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, roughly how many volunteers help to keep the National Trust running?',array['Over 600,000','About 6,100','More than 61,000','Around 16,000']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The handbook says more than 61,000 volunteers now support the National Trust''s work.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which parts of the UK does the National Trust (rather than the National Trust for Scotland) look after places of interest?',array['England, Wales and Northern Ireland','England and Wales only','England only','England, Scotland and Wales']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The National Trust covers England, Wales and Northern Ireland, while Scotland has its own National Trust for Scotland.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, how old is the clock at the Houses of Parliament often called Big Ben?',array['Over 500 years old','Over 300 years old','Over 150 years old','Over 50 years old']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The handbook describes the parliamentary clock as more than 150 years old and a popular sight for tourists.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What are the ''biomes'' at the Eden Project?',array['Underground caves showing the history of tin mining','Giant greenhouse-like structures housing plants from around the world','Aquariums holding sea creatures from Cornish waters','Museums of Roman remains found in the south west']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Eden Project''s biomes work like enormous greenhouses and contain plants gathered from all over the world.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: As well as being a visitor attraction, the Eden Project is a charity that runs environmental and social projects around the world.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Eden Project in Cornwall is also a charity carrying out environmental and social work internationally.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which organisation looks after Edinburgh Castle?',array['Historic Scotland','The National Trust for Scotland','The National Trust','The Eden Project']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Edinburgh Castle is cared for by Historic Scotland, an agency of the Scottish government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The history of Edinburgh Castle goes back to:',array['the early Middle Ages','the Victorian era','the Roman occupation','the Tudor period']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Edinburgh Castle, which dominates the city skyline, has a history stretching back to the early Middle Ages.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What kind of body is Historic Scotland?',array['A private company','A charity founded by three volunteers','A Scottish government agency','A department of the UK Parliament']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Historic Scotland, which looks after Edinburgh Castle, is an agency of the Scottish government.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the Giant''s Causeway made of?',array['Sandstone arches carved by rivers','Granite blocks placed by Stone Age people','Chalk cliffs shaped by the sea','Columns formed from volcanic lava']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Giant''s Causeway is a natural formation of columns created from volcanic lava.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Roughly how long ago was the Giant''s Causeway formed?',array['About 5 billion years ago','About 500,000 years ago','About 50 million years ago','About 5,000 years ago']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Causeway formed around 50 million years ago, and many legends describe how it came about.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-018','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where is Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park?',array['In the west of Scotland','In the north west of England','In North Wales','In the east of Scotland']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): This national park lies in the west of Scotland and contains Loch Lomond.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How large is Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park?',array['885 square miles','838 square miles','720 square miles','1,200 square miles']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Its area is 720 square miles, which is about 1,865 square kilometres.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How tall is the London Eye?',array['1,043 feet (318 metres)','643 feet (196 metres)','443 feet (135 metres)','243 feet (74 metres)']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The London Eye Ferris wheel stands 443 feet, or 135 metres, high.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The London Eye still plays an important part in New Year celebrations.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Built for the millennium, the London Eye remains a key feature of New Year festivities.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which part of the UK is Snowdonia National Park?',array['The Scottish Highlands','Cumbria in England','South Wales','North Wales']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Snowdonia is a national park located in the north of Wales.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-023','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the handbook, how large is Snowdonia National Park?',array['720 square miles','885 square miles','538 square miles','838 square miles']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Snowdonia covers 838 square miles (2,170 square kilometres).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-024','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Visitors to the Tower of London can see the Crown Jewels.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Besides tours by the Yeoman Warders, the Tower of London lets people view the Crown Jewels.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-025','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the biggest stretch of water in the Lake District?',array['Windermere','Loch Lomond','Coniston Water','Wastwater']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Windermere is the largest body of water in the Lake District.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-026','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In 2007, television viewers voted which place as Britain''s favourite view?',array['Snowdon','Loch Lomond','Windermere','Wastwater']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): A 2007 vote by TV viewers chose Wastwater, in the Lake District, as Britain''s favourite view.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-027','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How large is the Lake District National Park?',array['720 square miles','838 square miles','885 square miles','585 square miles']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): England''s biggest national park, the Lake District, covers 885 square miles (2,292 square kilometres).',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.5-028','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','The Lake District is famous for which TWO features?',array['Lakes and mountains','Castles and cathedrals','Forests and caves','Beaches and sea cliffs']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Lake District is known for its lakes and mountains and attracts many climbers, walkers and sailors.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Historically, the UK is a Christian country.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Britain has historically been Christian, although many other faiths are practised today.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people identified themselves as Christian?',array['70%','85%','60%','50%']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Seven in ten people described themselves as Christian in the 2009 Citizenship Survey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people identified themselves as Muslim?',array['4%','10%','1%','15%']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Muslims made up 4% of respondents in the 2009 Citizenship Survey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people identified themselves as Hindu?',array['8%','0.5%','2%','12%']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Hindus accounted for 2% of people in the 2009 Citizenship Survey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which group made up about 1% of people in the 2009 Citizenship Survey?',array['Christians','Sikhs','People with no religion','Muslims']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sikhs were about 1% of the population in the 2009 survey, compared with 70% Christian, 4% Muslim and 21% no religion.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, which TWO religions were each followed by less than 0.5% of people?',array['Judaism and Buddhism','Islam and Hinduism','Hinduism and Buddhism','Sikhism and Islam']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Jewish and Buddhist people each made up under half of one per cent in the 2009 survey.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the 2009 Citizenship Survey, what percentage of people said they had no religion?',array['40%','21%','5%','70%']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Just over a fifth of people (21%) reported having no religion.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the place of worship of Sikhs called?',array['A gurdwara','A synagogue','A mosque','A cathedral']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Sikh gurdwaras are among the religious buildings found throughout the UK.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-009','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is a Jewish place of worship called?',array['A gurdwara','A mosque','A synagogue','A temple']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Jewish synagogues are found across the UK, along with mosques, temples and gurdwaras.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-010','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which part of the UK is there a constitutional link between Church and state?',array['Scotland','Northern Ireland','England','Wales']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): England has a constitutional connection between Church and state, with the Church of England as the official Church.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-011','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the Church of England called in Scotland and the United States?',array['The Methodist Church','The Episcopal Church','The Church of Scotland','The Presbyterian Church']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Elsewhere the Church of England is known as the Anglican Church, and in Scotland and the USA as the Episcopal Church.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-012','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In countries outside the UK, the Church of England is generally known as:',array['the Anglican Church','the Catholic Church','the Presbyterian Church','the Baptist Church']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Outside the UK, the Church of England is called the Anglican Church.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-013','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Since when has the Church of England existed?',array['Since the Norman Conquest in 1066','Since the Glorious Revolution of 1688','Since the Reformation in the 1530s','Since the Act of Union in 1707']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Church of England is a Protestant Church dating from the Reformation of the 1530s.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-014','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who usually makes the choice of the Archbishop of Canterbury and other senior Church of England officials?',array['The House of Commons by a free vote','The Prime Minister together with a Church-appointed committee','Church members in a national election','The Moderator of the Church of Scotland']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The monarch has the right to choose senior church officials, but in practice the Prime Minister and a Church-appointed committee usually decide.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-015','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How is the Church of Scotland governed?',array['By the Scottish Parliament','By ministers and elders','By the monarch and bishops','By the Archbishop of Canterbury']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): The Church of Scotland, a Presbyterian Church, is run by its ministers and elders.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-016','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Apart from the Protestant Churches, which is the biggest denomination of Christianity in the UK?',array['Methodist','Baptist','Quaker','Roman Catholic']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Beyond Protestant groups, other Christian denominations exist, the largest being Roman Catholic.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-017','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which TWO are Protestant Christian groups in the UK?',array['Roman Catholics and Baptists','Baptists and Quakers','Roman Catholics and Quakers','Hindus and Methodists']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Baptists, Methodists, Presbyterians and Quakers are Protestant groups; Roman Catholics form a separate denomination.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-019','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','On which date is St Andrew''s Day?',array['23 April','1 March','17 March','30 November']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St Andrew''s Day, Scotland''s patron saint''s day, falls on 30 November.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-020','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who is the patron saint of Wales?',array['St George','St David','St Patrick','St Andrew']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Each UK nation has a patron saint; Wales''s is St David, honoured on 1 March.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-021','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: In Scotland, all businesses and offices close on St Andrew''s Day.',array['True','False']::text[],'B','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): St Andrew''s Day is an official holiday in Scotland, but not every business and office closes.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.6-022','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How are patron saints'' days still celebrated in England and Wales?',array['With a national two-minute silence','With a compulsory day off for all workers','With parades and small festivals','With an address by the Archbishop of Canterbury']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Although England and Wales no longer give a public holiday for these days, parades and small festivals still mark them.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-001','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which city is Wembley Stadium?',array['London','Cardiff','Birmingham','Manchester']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Wembley Stadium in London is one of the UK''s major sporting venues.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-002','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: Lawn tennis and golf are among the famous sports that began in Britain.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Cricket, football, lawn tennis, golf and rugby all started in Britain.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-003','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Who provides sports facilities such as swimming pools, tennis courts and dry ski slopes in the UK?',array['Only the national government','Only the Church of England','Only schools and universities','Local governments and private companies']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Facilities like pools, gyms, pitches and dry ski slopes are provided by local councils and private businesses.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-004','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Where did the British team finish in the medal table at the 2012 Olympic Games?',array['Fifth','First','Second','Third']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Team GB did well in many Olympic sports in 2012 and came third in the medal table.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-005','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','TRUE or FALSE: The 2012 Paralympic Games were held in London.',array['True','False']::text[],'A','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): London hosted the Paralympic Games as well as the Olympics in 2012.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-006','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Dr Sir Ludwig Guttmann, whose work led to the Paralympics, was:',array['an American army surgeon','a Scottish rugby player','a German refugee','a French Olympic champion']::text[],'C','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Guttmann, a refugee from Germany, worked at Stoke Mandeville hospital, where the Paralympics have their origins.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-007','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In which county is Stoke Mandeville hospital, where the Paralympics have their origin?',array['Cornwall','Berkshire','Suffolk','Buckinghamshire']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Stoke Mandeville hospital is in Buckinghamshire.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
  ('lituk:S4.7-008','LIFE_IN_UK'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Dr Guttmann developed new methods of treatment for people with which kind of injury?',array['Sight loss','Head injuries','Burns','Spinal injuries']::text[],'D','Handbook ch. 4 (A modern, thriving society): Guttmann pioneered treatment for spinal injuries and got patients involved in exercise and sport.',array['life_in_uk','lituk_society']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-05',null,null,null,null,'practice'),
